1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,400

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,920
I'll be introducing
our speaker for today.

3
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:13,000
So Andrew Lampinen is a member
of technical staff at Anthropic.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,760
And previously, he was a
staff research scientist

5
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,680
at Google DeepMind and received
his PhD in cognitive psychology

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:21,240
here at Stanford.

7
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:23,920
His research interests
bridge AI and cognitive

8
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:28,120
science on topics including
learning, generalization,

9
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:31,480
and representation in language
models, agents, and humans,

10
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:34,280
as well as broader
issues methodologies

11
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:36,440
for generalizable research.

12
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:40,160
And feel free to check
out his Substack for more.

13
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:44,180
And without further ado,
I'll hand it off to Andrew.

14
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:47,470
[APPLAUSE]

15
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:48,880

16
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:52,480
So I think a question that's
motivated a lot of my work

17
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:55,640
over the years is the question
of what computational principles

18
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,922
are shared across natural
and artificial intelligence?

19
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:01,380
And that's a question that I
want to touch on in this talk,

20
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:03,297
even though this is a
class about transformers

21
00:01:03,297 --> 00:01:05,519
or a seminar series
about transformers.

22
00:01:05,519 --> 00:01:09,120
And in particular, the question
that I want to think about here

23
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:12,140
is how do language models
generalize from what they learn?

24
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:14,800
And is there anything from
that could tell us something

25
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:18,200
about how the systems of
natural intelligence work,

26
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:23,120
the different ways that
natural intelligences learned?

27
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:24,960
And this work started
from observing

28
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:28,400
that there's two different ways
that language models can learn.

29
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,180
This is probably not
surprising to you.

30
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,720
Of course, one way they learn
is that over a large training

31
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:35,520
corpus, we optimize the
network's parameters in order

32
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:37,800
to get them to encode some
knowledge or some skills

33
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:40,000
that we want them to have.

34
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,600
But the other thing,
one of the things

35
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:43,740
that got people most excited
about language models

36
00:01:43,740 --> 00:01:45,240
in the first place
was their ability

37
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:47,060
to learn from
information in context.

38
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:49,180
You can just put some
examples of a task

39
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:52,200
or some facts or some documents
into the language model's

40
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,800
context, and the models
can use that information

41
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,720
to learn new things, sometimes
even to learn new languages

42
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:01,910
or do fairly sophisticated
things like that.

43
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:05,030
And the cool thing about
doing cognitive science

44
00:02:05,030 --> 00:02:06,470
on models like
this is that we can

45
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:08,110
do all kinds of
controlled experiments

46
00:02:08,110 --> 00:02:11,050
that would be very hard to
do on natural intelligences,

47
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:13,130
like we can take
novel information.

48
00:02:13,130 --> 00:02:15,730
We can put it into
these different sources,

49
00:02:15,730 --> 00:02:18,750
and we can ask how the
system uses that information

50
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:21,750
to make predictions in a
response to further questions

51
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:24,470
or tasks or so on.

52
00:02:24,470 --> 00:02:26,230
And the experiments
I'm going to tell you

53
00:02:26,230 --> 00:02:30,883
about today come
from doing this work.

54
00:02:30,883 --> 00:02:32,550
And in particular,
I'm going to tell you

55
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:35,830
about a series of works we've
done where we found some pretty

56
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:38,590
striking differences in
how these models generalize

57
00:02:38,590 --> 00:02:41,550
from the information that's
stored in their parameters

58
00:02:41,550 --> 00:02:43,670
and from information in context.

59
00:02:43,670 --> 00:02:47,150
And then after that, we came to
studying several paths to bridge

60
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:49,310
this generalization gap.

61
00:02:49,310 --> 00:02:51,870
And at the end, I'll come back
to talk about these bigger

62
00:02:51,870 --> 00:02:53,950
picture questions about
how these findings might

63
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:57,030
relate to natural intelligence.

64
00:02:57,030 --> 00:02:58,590
And if you're
interested, the things

65
00:02:58,590 --> 00:03:00,510
I'm going to talk
about today come mostly

66
00:03:00,510 --> 00:03:03,063
from this series of three
works on the generalization

67
00:03:03,063 --> 00:03:05,230
of language models from
in-context learning and fine

68
00:03:05,230 --> 00:03:08,270
tuning, latent learning, and
improving latent generalization

69
00:03:08,270 --> 00:03:09,690
using test time compute.

70
00:03:09,690 --> 00:03:12,870

71
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:13,470
Awesome.

72
00:03:13,470 --> 00:03:15,710
So let's dive in by
talking about learning

73
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:17,730
in parameters and in context.

74
00:03:17,730 --> 00:03:21,070

75
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:22,710
So the inspiration
for this work really

76
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:25,370
started from this nice
paper, The Reversal Curse.

77
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:29,070
Have you seen this paper
or heard of this paper?

78
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:31,070
Not too many, OK.

79
00:03:31,070 --> 00:03:34,430
So this was a paper
from Owin Evans' group.

80
00:03:34,430 --> 00:03:37,030
And what they showed is that
if you take a language model

81
00:03:37,030 --> 00:03:39,650
and you fine tune it on
some facts in one order,

82
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:41,630
like you tell it,
Daphne Barrington

83
00:03:41,630 --> 00:03:44,350
is the director of A
Journey Through Time,

84
00:03:44,350 --> 00:03:46,150
if you then test that
model on a question

85
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:47,650
like who is Daphne Barrington?

86
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:49,750
The model will do quite well.

87
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:52,670
But if you ask it
the question that

88
00:03:52,670 --> 00:03:54,730
forces it to reverse
the relation,

89
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:57,700
like who is the person who
directed A Journey Through Time,

90
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:00,750
the model has quite a hard
time answering that question.

91
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:03,000
So this is what the authors
termed the reversal curse,

92
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,300
that models are tied to the
direction in which they've

93
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:07,400
learned a relation.

94
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:09,120
They can't easily reverse it.

95
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:12,340

96
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:15,220
But the thing that surprised
us about seeing this work

97
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:19,240
is that if you try this
experiment in the chat,

98
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:21,519
for example, the models
are perfectly fine at it.

99
00:04:21,519 --> 00:04:23,640
If you give them
some made up fact,

100
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:26,100
like Z packs are
bigger than carpools,

101
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:30,360
then carpools are smaller than Z
packs, this was actually-- well,

102
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:31,360
this is a reversal.

103
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:34,940
And in a paper we did earlier
with much simpler language

104
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:37,660
models, this was our
easy baseline condition

105
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:41,740
that all the models were
basically at ceiling at.

106
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:44,300
And so this made us
ask the question is

107
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:46,820
it possible that the way
that models generalize

108
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:49,740
from information that they
encounter in their context

109
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:52,060
is different from how they
generalize from information

110
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:53,720
they've stored in
their parameters?

111
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:57,140

112
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:58,855
And this would be an
interesting finding

113
00:04:58,855 --> 00:05:00,980
because there's actually
a lot of theoretical works

114
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:04,180
that have argued that
in-context learning is basically

115
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:07,260
doing some form of
approximate gradient descent,

116
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:09,220
at least in settings
where gradient descent is

117
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:10,600
the optimal thing to do.

118
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:13,620

119
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:15,780
So the question we asked
in this first paper

120
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:18,900
was, which generalizes
better, a language model

121
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,380
that's been fine-tuned
on a new data set or one

122
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:23,960
that's got the whole
data set in its context?

123
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:27,207
So what we did is we took
various data sets that contain,

124
00:05:27,207 --> 00:05:29,540
let's say, thousands of
documents, hundreds of thousands

125
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:31,500
of tokens, and we
looked at different ways

126
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:35,020
that we could teach the language
model about this information.

127
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:37,120
The first way is that you
just take the data set,

128
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:40,820
you fine-tune the model
on it in the standard way.

129
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:43,760
And the second way, now that we
have fairly long context models,

130
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:47,280
is that you can just
concatenate that whole data set,

131
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:49,100
give the model
document separators

132
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:53,100
and put the entire
data set in context.

133
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:54,960
And then in both
of these settings,

134
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:57,660
we're going to test how the
model makes generalizations

135
00:05:57,660 --> 00:05:59,400
from the data in this data set.

136
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:02,060
For example, reversals
of relations but also

137
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:04,380
more complex kind
of structures like

138
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:08,020
syllogistic generalizations.

139
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:10,980
Does that make sense?

140
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:11,828
Cool.

141
00:06:11,828 --> 00:06:13,620
So I'm first going to
show you some results

142
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:15,900
on the original
reversal curse data set.

143
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:17,660
This is us replicating
the findings

144
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:19,580
from that work on
a different model.

145
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:22,160
So if you look at the
reversal accuracy,

146
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:24,180
a pre-trained
model is at chance.

147
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:26,700
When you fine-tune the model
on the relations in only one

148
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:28,540
direction, it actually
gets slightly worse

149
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:31,220
than chance on these reversals.

150
00:06:31,220 --> 00:06:34,140
But if you just put the
entire data set in context,

151
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:39,060
the model is able to answer the
reversals with 99% accuracy.

152
00:06:39,060 --> 00:06:41,740
So there's a big gap
between the model's ability

153
00:06:41,740 --> 00:06:44,560
to generalize from information
that's fine-tuned on

154
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:46,980
and its ability to generalize
that kind of information

155
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:47,920
in context.

156
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:52,020

157
00:06:52,020 --> 00:06:54,710
We also tested other kinds
of structures in this paper,

158
00:06:54,710 --> 00:06:57,290
for example, syllogistic
generalization.

159
00:06:57,290 --> 00:07:00,210
So syllogisms, you
might from classic logic

160
00:07:00,210 --> 00:07:04,790
are these sets of
two propositions,

161
00:07:04,790 --> 00:07:07,570
like all zamp are snaff,
no snaff are plusk,

162
00:07:07,570 --> 00:07:09,830
that logically imply
a third proposition,

163
00:07:09,830 --> 00:07:11,850
like no zamp are plusk.

164
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:16,835
We set up these syllogisms
with these nonsense nouns.

165
00:07:16,835 --> 00:07:18,210
So we knew there
were things that

166
00:07:18,210 --> 00:07:19,970
weren't in the training data.

167
00:07:19,970 --> 00:07:23,050
And we just looked at if we
train the models on documents

168
00:07:23,050 --> 00:07:25,110
that contain
information like this,

169
00:07:25,110 --> 00:07:28,090
will they be able to generalize
to the logical implications

170
00:07:28,090 --> 00:07:30,330
of those things?

171
00:07:30,330 --> 00:07:32,810
And here, again, we see,
well, pre-trained models

172
00:07:32,810 --> 00:07:35,410
are at chance because these
are all made up facts.

173
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:38,190
If we fine-tuned models on
these kinds of documents,

174
00:07:38,190 --> 00:07:41,290
they do get a little bit of
above chance generalization

175
00:07:41,290 --> 00:07:44,090
to the held out tests,
but not very much.

176
00:07:44,090 --> 00:07:45,690
And in-context
learning, where we just

177
00:07:45,690 --> 00:07:48,050
take the entire data set of
thousands of these documents

178
00:07:48,050 --> 00:07:49,610
and put it in
context, is actually

179
00:07:49,610 --> 00:07:51,150
able to generalize better.

180
00:07:51,150 --> 00:07:54,810

181
00:07:54,810 --> 00:07:57,970
So these are two examples of
how in-context learning can

182
00:07:57,970 --> 00:07:59,930
generalize better
than fine-tuning

183
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:01,750
to certain kinds of tests.

184
00:08:01,750 --> 00:08:06,490

185
00:08:06,490 --> 00:08:08,810
Of course, it's natural
to ask why this is.

186
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:11,730
And I think the answer
is that there's actually

187
00:08:11,730 --> 00:08:13,970
a lot of structures on--

188
00:08:13,970 --> 00:08:16,070
examples of these
structures on the internet

189
00:08:16,070 --> 00:08:16,990
in the training data.

190
00:08:16,990 --> 00:08:20,670
People reversing a
relation in a document

191
00:08:20,670 --> 00:08:23,390
in an essay in a logical
argument, of course,

192
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:25,330
is actually a
pretty common thing.

193
00:08:25,330 --> 00:08:27,050
And so it's natural
for the models

194
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:31,770
to learn how to use these
kinds of structures in context.

195
00:08:31,770 --> 00:08:34,409
But then the question is,
of course, why don't they

196
00:08:34,409 --> 00:08:38,429
learn how to generalize them
appropriately from fine-tuning?

197
00:08:38,429 --> 00:08:41,610

198
00:08:41,610 --> 00:08:44,410
Well, a natural possibility
that occurred to us

199
00:08:44,410 --> 00:08:47,490
was that maybe it is something
about fine-tuning itself.

200
00:08:47,490 --> 00:08:50,720
Maybe the models don't really
learn that much in fine-tuning.

201
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:52,937
Maybe if we pre-train
them from scratch,

202
00:08:52,937 --> 00:08:54,520
the models would be
able to generalize

203
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:59,120
these kinds of relations
more appropriately.

204
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:02,460
So to test that hypothesis, we
took a small language model,

205
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:06,280
one we could train from scratch,
and we created a large data set

206
00:09:06,280 --> 00:09:08,600
with 20,000 relationships.

207
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:10,860
With most of them, the
reversals were trained.

208
00:09:10,860 --> 00:09:13,120
But 1% of them were held out.

209
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:15,040
And the way this
data set looked there

210
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:18,800
was some prefixed data that
was just filler, basically,

211
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:20,280
for data diversity.

212
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:23,780
There's some trained relations
that are like X contain Y,

213
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:25,360
for example.

214
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:28,320
And there's also some in-context
learning training sequences,

215
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:32,880
where you get a forward like W
contains Z, and then a reverse,

216
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:36,560
Z are the subset of
W. And what we did

217
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,960
is that we held out a
few of the reversals

218
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,280
like Y is a subset
of X and we just

219
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:44,500
tested how well the models were
able to generalize to these.

220
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:47,280

221
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:50,640
And what we find is that if
you test the model's memory

222
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:53,380
for the trained relations,
even in a new context,

223
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:54,720
they do very well.

224
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:57,800
If you test their ability
to take one of the trained

225
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:00,560
relations for which the
reversal was held out

226
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:04,600
and reverse it in context,
they also do perfectly at that.

227
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:08,720
But they still get absolutely 0
generalization to the reversals.

228
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:11,420
So this is really not just
a phenomenon of fine-tuning.

229
00:10:11,420 --> 00:10:14,320
It's something more fundamental
about how the models generalize

230
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:16,700
from relational information
in their training data.

231
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:21,200

232
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:23,217
And it's not just
about reversals.

233
00:10:23,217 --> 00:10:24,800
We looked at other
kinds of structures

234
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:28,480
too, like this codebooks data
set, where the model is trained

235
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:30,480
on information about
how they encode

236
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:32,460
different kinds of
information in different--

237
00:10:32,460 --> 00:10:34,800
you can think of them
as different languages.

238
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:37,200
And then we give it
examples of using

239
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:39,380
some of the encoding
words from each language

240
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:40,260
but not all of them.

241
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:42,880
And then we test its ability
to use the other encoding

242
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:46,040
words that it hadn't
seen used in training.

243
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:48,000
And again, in this
context, we find

244
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,880
that the models are able to
recall the information well.

245
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:52,620
If the information
is in-context,

246
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:54,340
they're able to use it flexibly.

247
00:10:54,340 --> 00:10:56,600
But they're not able to
put those pieces together

248
00:10:56,600 --> 00:11:01,120
in order to generalize
to the held out tests.

249
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:02,180
Yes, there's a question.

250
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:02,680
[INAUDIBLE]

251
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:04,800
When you say no
context, you just

252
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:06,848
prompted to translate
the unknown thing,

253
00:11:06,848 --> 00:11:08,640
relying on the word
knowledge in the model?

254
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:09,140
Correct.

255
00:11:09,140 --> 00:11:13,597
Yeah, so basically, an example
of the no context sequences

256
00:11:13,597 --> 00:11:14,180
are like this.

257
00:11:14,180 --> 00:11:16,060
So we say codebook one.

258
00:11:16,060 --> 00:11:19,200
So that tells the model
which codebook it should use.

259
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:21,580
And then we give it the
sequence to translate

260
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:24,240
and we test how well it
generalizes to the test.

261
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:27,760
[INAUDIBLE] like, where
partially it did some

262
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:28,900
and partially it didn't?

263
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:31,970

264
00:11:31,970 --> 00:11:35,760
I don't think we have any
examples of that in the paper.

265
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:40,680
But I think it would basically
get the ones right that it knows

266
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:42,800
and the ones wrong
that it doesn't.

267
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:45,360
I should say that we do--

268
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:46,770
this is not shown on this graph.

269
00:11:46,770 --> 00:11:54,890
But we do have tests where
there's known encodings

270
00:11:54,890 --> 00:11:56,850
but in novel
sequences and stuff.

271
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:58,330
And it generalizes
well to those.

272
00:11:58,330 --> 00:11:59,247
So it's not just that.

273
00:11:59,247 --> 00:12:02,430
It's totally overfit to
the trained data set.

274
00:12:02,430 --> 00:12:03,030
Yes.

275
00:12:03,030 --> 00:12:03,850
Other questions?

276
00:12:03,850 --> 00:12:06,350
Just a quick question for
the interesting experiment

277
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:07,610
from the last slide.

278
00:12:07,610 --> 00:12:09,950
So I'm wondering
if you're trying

279
00:12:09,950 --> 00:12:13,790
to also gradually increase
the percentage of data

280
00:12:13,790 --> 00:12:18,670
that is a reversal and see where
do you get to the breakpoint.

281
00:12:18,670 --> 00:12:20,470
So basically is
it enough to just

282
00:12:20,470 --> 00:12:24,780
have 5%, 10% of
examples [INAUDIBLE]?

283
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:28,750
You mean for the
in-context learning part?

284
00:12:28,750 --> 00:12:30,070
Pre-training, right?

285
00:12:30,070 --> 00:12:31,570
Yeah.

286
00:12:31,570 --> 00:12:32,910
Yes.

287
00:12:32,910 --> 00:12:37,270
Yeah, that's a good question.

288
00:12:37,270 --> 00:12:39,870
Yeah, we didn't
try exactly that.

289
00:12:39,870 --> 00:12:48,070
I think the answer will depend
on other features of the data

290
00:12:48,070 --> 00:12:51,865
diversity because I
think it's about--

291
00:12:51,865 --> 00:12:53,490
we've seen this in
a lot of other work.

292
00:12:53,490 --> 00:12:55,590
It's about the relative
diversity of where

293
00:12:55,590 --> 00:12:57,922
you see reversal examples.

294
00:12:57,922 --> 00:12:59,630
You have to have a
certain minimal amount

295
00:12:59,630 --> 00:13:02,790
to learn how to do that
structural processing.

296
00:13:02,790 --> 00:13:04,910
But you also need some
amount of diversity

297
00:13:04,910 --> 00:13:07,110
to convince the model
that should generalize

298
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:08,483
beyond the training examples.

299
00:13:08,483 --> 00:13:10,150
So I don't know exactly
where the cutoff

300
00:13:10,150 --> 00:13:11,233
would be with this though.

301
00:13:11,233 --> 00:13:12,770
It's a good question.

302
00:13:12,770 --> 00:13:14,150
I have one more.

303
00:13:14,150 --> 00:13:14,670
Yes.

304
00:13:14,670 --> 00:13:19,410
Did you always encode these
propositions in English?

305
00:13:19,410 --> 00:13:22,070
Do you try doing it in
a more formal calculus?

306
00:13:22,070 --> 00:13:23,650
Is there any ambiguity?

307
00:13:23,650 --> 00:13:24,750
[INAUDIBLE]

308
00:13:24,750 --> 00:13:25,730
Yeah, great question.

309
00:13:25,730 --> 00:13:29,110
So for these models that are
trained-- so for the experiments

310
00:13:29,110 --> 00:13:32,350
on the pre-trained models, we
always did things in English.

311
00:13:32,350 --> 00:13:36,327
For these experiments where the
models are trained from scratch,

312
00:13:36,327 --> 00:13:38,910
the model doesn't anything about
English or any other language

313
00:13:38,910 --> 00:13:40,670
because we're training
it from scratch.

314
00:13:40,670 --> 00:13:41,610
So it's all tokens.

315
00:13:41,610 --> 00:13:44,190
But you can think
of them as being

316
00:13:44,190 --> 00:13:46,750
basically formal statements.

317
00:13:46,750 --> 00:13:50,830
You could replace this contain
token with a containment

318
00:13:50,830 --> 00:13:52,370
operator from math.

319
00:13:52,370 --> 00:13:59,190
And so you could think of these
as being formal statements.

320
00:13:59,190 --> 00:14:03,290
I don't think it makes
a big difference.

321
00:14:03,290 --> 00:14:07,550
Although, these
reversal things are--

322
00:14:07,550 --> 00:14:10,550
it could be that they are
formatting details that

323
00:14:10,550 --> 00:14:13,023
would make a difference here.

324
00:14:13,023 --> 00:14:15,190
Did you see the same behavior
across different model

325
00:14:15,190 --> 00:14:18,330
architectures or just
different popular--

326
00:14:18,330 --> 00:14:19,770
Yeah, so that's
a great question.

327
00:14:19,770 --> 00:14:23,790
So the short answer
is it depends.

328
00:14:23,790 --> 00:14:26,930
The reversal thing
in particular,

329
00:14:26,930 --> 00:14:28,710
there's been a lot
of work on this,

330
00:14:28,710 --> 00:14:33,510
mostly not by us
but by other people.

331
00:14:33,510 --> 00:14:36,310
And here are some
things that fix it.

332
00:14:36,310 --> 00:14:38,850
Having a bidirectional
transformer fixes it.

333
00:14:38,850 --> 00:14:40,630
That's obvious.

334
00:14:40,630 --> 00:14:45,220
There are also things you can do
that are changing the learning

335
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:48,395
objective that also fix it.

336
00:14:48,395 --> 00:14:50,020
I'll maybe come back
to this at the end

337
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:53,660
because it relates to
some of the things I'll

338
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:54,940
talk about later.

339
00:14:54,940 --> 00:15:00,180
But, yeah, it's definitely
not architecture independent,

340
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:04,500
but it is a pretty systematic
feature of architectures

341
00:15:04,500 --> 00:15:07,780
that do causal next token
prediction without doing

342
00:15:07,780 --> 00:15:10,360
some trick to avoid it,
I guess I would say.

343
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:13,780

344
00:15:13,780 --> 00:15:14,580
Awesome.

345
00:15:14,580 --> 00:15:15,540
Cool.

346
00:15:15,540 --> 00:15:18,620
Let me move on.

347
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:20,000
So this is two examples.

348
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:21,540
There's more in
the paper showing

349
00:15:21,540 --> 00:15:24,400
that even if you train
a model from scratch,

350
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:25,980
there's certain
kinds of structures

351
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:29,300
that the models just
fail to generalize even

352
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:32,100
when they have enough data
to support them generalizing

353
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:32,622
in context.

354
00:15:32,622 --> 00:15:34,580
So we're really confident
that they're learning

355
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:36,360
these structures from the data.

356
00:15:36,360 --> 00:15:39,780

357
00:15:39,780 --> 00:15:42,980
And the way I've been
thinking about this

358
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:46,740
more recently is that there's
many types of training data

359
00:15:46,740 --> 00:15:49,900
that have explicit information
but also convey something

360
00:15:49,900 --> 00:15:50,480
latently.

361
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:52,525
So you can think of the
reversals as like this.

362
00:15:52,525 --> 00:15:53,900
There's some
explicit information

363
00:15:53,900 --> 00:15:56,700
which is a forward relation,
like X is Y's parent.

364
00:15:56,700 --> 00:16:00,980
There's some latent information
like who Y's parent was.

365
00:16:00,980 --> 00:16:04,260
The multi-hop
syllogism-like data.

366
00:16:04,260 --> 00:16:07,940
Again, there's multiple explicit
statements, like X is Y's parent

367
00:16:07,940 --> 00:16:09,073
and Y is Z's parent.

368
00:16:09,073 --> 00:16:10,740
And then there's some
latent information

369
00:16:10,740 --> 00:16:15,262
that they imply like Z
is the grandchild of X.

370
00:16:15,262 --> 00:16:17,220
There's other structures
where things like this

371
00:16:17,220 --> 00:16:19,180
have been observed, where
models aren't always

372
00:16:19,180 --> 00:16:22,340
good at generalizing, like
cross-lingual generalization

373
00:16:22,340 --> 00:16:25,180
or this generalization to
alternative goals, which

374
00:16:25,180 --> 00:16:27,940
is something we think
about in the paper.

375
00:16:27,940 --> 00:16:32,220
So I think that when models
are exposed to information that

376
00:16:32,220 --> 00:16:35,100
conveys some underlying
latent information,

377
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:38,180
they won't always be able to
generalize that information

378
00:16:38,180 --> 00:16:43,650
effectively if they've just been
trained on the explicit bits.

379
00:16:43,650 --> 00:16:47,610
And what I think-- so to
try to sketch this out

380
00:16:47,610 --> 00:16:50,250
in a different way, if you
think of the training documents,

381
00:16:50,250 --> 00:16:52,310
when we do parametric
learning on the model,

382
00:16:52,310 --> 00:16:55,270
when we train information
into its parameters,

383
00:16:55,270 --> 00:16:57,410
it consolidates
information that integrates

384
00:16:57,410 --> 00:17:00,690
across a lot of documents,
but it's a bit more

385
00:17:00,690 --> 00:17:03,090
tied to how that
information was explicitly

386
00:17:03,090 --> 00:17:04,790
conveyed in the documents.

387
00:17:04,790 --> 00:17:07,290
And that means that the model
is less flexible in using

388
00:17:07,290 --> 00:17:09,430
that information at test time.

389
00:17:09,430 --> 00:17:12,630
By contrast, when we do
in-context learning, of course,

390
00:17:12,630 --> 00:17:15,790
you can fit relatively
little information in there,

391
00:17:15,790 --> 00:17:18,770
but it's available in richer
detail in a way that can be more

392
00:17:18,770 --> 00:17:22,010
flexibly used at test time.

393
00:17:22,010 --> 00:17:25,690
Does that distinction
make sense?

394
00:17:25,690 --> 00:17:29,730
OK, awesome.

395
00:17:29,730 --> 00:17:30,410
Yes.

396
00:17:30,410 --> 00:17:32,730
And I do want to
convey, though, that

397
00:17:32,730 --> 00:17:36,250
this consolidated information
is important for the model's

398
00:17:36,250 --> 00:17:37,030
generalization.

399
00:17:37,030 --> 00:17:40,370
It is very much the
parametric information

400
00:17:40,370 --> 00:17:43,090
that's allowing the models to
learn how to use information

401
00:17:43,090 --> 00:17:44,690
more flexibly at test time.

402
00:17:44,690 --> 00:17:47,290
So I'm definitely not saying
that the parametric learning

403
00:17:47,290 --> 00:17:48,970
is unimportant.

404
00:17:48,970 --> 00:17:51,010
And indeed, the way
that I think about this

405
00:17:51,010 --> 00:17:52,890
is that in-context
learning is better

406
00:17:52,890 --> 00:17:56,090
at using specific pieces
of information flexibly.

407
00:17:56,090 --> 00:17:57,730
But parametric
learning is better

408
00:17:57,730 --> 00:17:59,810
at extracting statistical
structures that

409
00:17:59,810 --> 00:18:03,228
are common across many
different documents.

410
00:18:03,228 --> 00:18:04,770
And those include
the structures that

411
00:18:04,770 --> 00:18:07,770
support flexible in-context
learning and the structures that

412
00:18:07,770 --> 00:18:09,975
support other kinds
of generalization.

413
00:18:09,975 --> 00:18:11,350
And if you're
interested in that,

414
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:13,890
I think you can see some nice
examples in this paper, Deep

415
00:18:13,890 --> 00:18:17,797
Sequence Models Tend to
Memorize Geometrically.

416
00:18:17,797 --> 00:18:19,630
I don't agree with
everything in this paper,

417
00:18:19,630 --> 00:18:23,370
but I think it shows
some nice examples of how

418
00:18:23,370 --> 00:18:25,330
statistical learning
across many documents

419
00:18:25,330 --> 00:18:29,930
can extract a statistical
structure that is actually

420
00:18:29,930 --> 00:18:32,270
harder for models
to use in context.

421
00:18:32,270 --> 00:18:35,710

422
00:18:35,710 --> 00:18:36,210
Cool.

423
00:18:36,210 --> 00:18:39,170

424
00:18:39,170 --> 00:18:42,850
I'll show you one example
because I think this is actually

425
00:18:42,850 --> 00:18:46,250
an important reason that
these failures of latent

426
00:18:46,250 --> 00:18:49,170
generalization are not
so obvious unless you

427
00:18:49,170 --> 00:18:50,850
do controlled experiments.

428
00:18:50,850 --> 00:18:52,590
And the example works like this.

429
00:18:52,590 --> 00:18:55,090
So language models
often generalize better

430
00:18:55,090 --> 00:18:56,750
than 0% in practice.

431
00:18:56,750 --> 00:18:59,237
And this latent
generalization story

432
00:18:59,237 --> 00:19:01,570
would suggest that they're
really missing all the latent

433
00:19:01,570 --> 00:19:03,330
structure in the data.

434
00:19:03,330 --> 00:19:06,170
So, for example,
if we train models

435
00:19:06,170 --> 00:19:08,130
on something like
our syllogism tests

436
00:19:08,130 --> 00:19:10,770
on statements all
birds have wings,

437
00:19:10,770 --> 00:19:12,970
eagles are a type
of bird, and then

438
00:19:12,970 --> 00:19:14,370
test them on the
statement eagles

439
00:19:14,370 --> 00:19:16,290
have wings, that
would probably be

440
00:19:16,290 --> 00:19:19,050
a hard generalization for them
to make from their parameters,

441
00:19:19,050 --> 00:19:21,210
easier in context.

442
00:19:21,210 --> 00:19:23,970
But if as in natural
training data,

443
00:19:23,970 --> 00:19:28,010
the model would get lots of
other examples of words that

444
00:19:28,010 --> 00:19:31,258
tend to co-occur
together, like eagles fly,

445
00:19:31,258 --> 00:19:33,050
and pigeons are a type
of bird, and pigeons

446
00:19:33,050 --> 00:19:35,425
have wings and pigeons fly,
and hawks are a type of bird.

447
00:19:35,425 --> 00:19:37,440
And Hawks have
wings and Hawks fly.

448
00:19:37,440 --> 00:19:41,200
Then suddenly, this test
statement that eagles have wings

449
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:42,700
might not be so hard.

450
00:19:42,700 --> 00:19:44,240
The models might
be able to infer it

451
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:46,720
from word co-occurrences alone.

452
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:49,680
And indeed, this is what we find
in some tests in these papers,

453
00:19:49,680 --> 00:19:53,840
that if you give the model
enough statistical structure,

454
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:57,800
it can skip the need for
doing systematic reversals

455
00:19:57,800 --> 00:19:59,920
or syllogistic
inferences and just

456
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:02,880
generalize based on the
word co-occurrences alone.

457
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:05,800
So this is an example of
how parametric learning can

458
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:09,000
do types of generalization
that work around its failures

459
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:13,800
to do more structured
relational generalization.

460
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:15,440
Does that make sense?

461
00:20:15,440 --> 00:20:17,120
That can go horribly
wrong, right?

462
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:19,640
Oh, yeah.

463
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:20,560
Totally.

464
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:21,420
So--

465
00:20:21,420 --> 00:20:24,580
Meaning this would prove it
would learn nonsense things,

466
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:26,560
just word co-occurrences?

467
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:27,080
Correct.

468
00:20:27,080 --> 00:20:34,960
Yeah, so I think the statistics
are useful in expectation.

469
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:36,440
And so the models
will learn things

470
00:20:36,440 --> 00:20:37,860
that are useful in expectation.

471
00:20:37,860 --> 00:20:39,560
But in any particular
instance, those

472
00:20:39,560 --> 00:20:41,420
can lead to incorrect
generalizations.

473
00:20:41,420 --> 00:20:47,620
But I think this is a general
phenomenon of learning systems,

474
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:49,400
I might be so bold as to say.

475
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:51,730
It's also the case
that, in-context,

476
00:20:51,730 --> 00:20:52,980
the models will make mistakes.

477
00:20:52,980 --> 00:20:55,760
It's also the case that when
you teach humans something,

478
00:20:55,760 --> 00:20:57,160
they will make
mistakes about how

479
00:20:57,160 --> 00:20:58,460
to generalize that information.

480
00:20:58,460 --> 00:21:00,920
It's just a really hard
problem to get everything right

481
00:21:00,920 --> 00:21:01,700
all the time.

482
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:04,117
I'm just wondering, though,
if the word co-occurrences are

483
00:21:04,117 --> 00:21:07,520
boosted by some glimmer of
latent understanding better

484
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:08,260
than chance.

485
00:21:08,260 --> 00:21:10,120
Or is it--

486
00:21:10,120 --> 00:21:12,280
Well, so I do
really think this is

487
00:21:12,280 --> 00:21:14,352
a legitimate mode
of generalization,

488
00:21:14,352 --> 00:21:15,060
just to be clear.

489
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:18,320
I think the model should
be integrating information

490
00:21:18,320 --> 00:21:21,440
across these examples
to infer that things

491
00:21:21,440 --> 00:21:24,720
that are birds and fly
typically have wings.

492
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:26,880
I think that's a
legitimate generalization.

493
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:31,440
Now, it's interesting because
if you took a slightly different

494
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:33,710
example, like
penguins fly, where

495
00:21:33,710 --> 00:21:35,390
these co-occurrence
structures would

496
00:21:35,390 --> 00:21:39,970
lead to that generalization,
maybe it's not licensed there.

497
00:21:39,970 --> 00:21:42,650
And you will see that
models get it wrong.

498
00:21:42,650 --> 00:21:45,070
And also young children
actually get these statements

499
00:21:45,070 --> 00:21:46,670
wrong some of the time.

500
00:21:46,670 --> 00:21:50,930
But as we develop more refined
understanding of the world,

501
00:21:50,930 --> 00:21:51,775
we can repair that.

502
00:21:51,775 --> 00:21:53,650
And I think it's very
similar for the models.

503
00:21:53,650 --> 00:21:57,710
This statistical learning
is helpful on average.

504
00:21:57,710 --> 00:21:59,950
It will get certain
kinds of things wrong,

505
00:21:59,950 --> 00:22:03,030
but you just need
more data to fix that.

506
00:22:03,030 --> 00:22:04,130
It's the short answer.

507
00:22:04,130 --> 00:22:06,630
Of course, the models might be
able to do more sophisticated

508
00:22:06,630 --> 00:22:09,110
things in context or using
other information sources

509
00:22:09,110 --> 00:22:10,270
or methods, et cetera.

510
00:22:10,270 --> 00:22:12,230
Does that make sense?

511
00:22:12,230 --> 00:22:13,190
Yes.

512
00:22:13,190 --> 00:22:15,810
Can we think of
this as compression?

513
00:22:15,810 --> 00:22:18,390
When we see a statement
in the forwards direction,

514
00:22:18,390 --> 00:22:20,970
that's useful because I've
seen in the training set.

515
00:22:20,970 --> 00:22:24,950
But the reverse is lost in
compression parametrically.

516
00:22:24,950 --> 00:22:27,870
Yeah, I think you
can think about it

517
00:22:27,870 --> 00:22:30,770
in that way to some extent.

518
00:22:30,770 --> 00:22:38,890
I think it's tricky
because, at some level,

519
00:22:38,890 --> 00:22:43,590
what I want to argue is that--

520
00:22:43,590 --> 00:22:47,590
I think there's a
story which this

521
00:22:47,590 --> 00:22:51,270
supports that compression can
be useful for generalization.

522
00:22:51,270 --> 00:22:54,530
But I think there's also cases
where the compression loses

523
00:22:54,530 --> 00:22:56,530
information that's important
for generalization.

524
00:22:56,530 --> 00:22:58,322
And I think that's what
you're pointing to.

525
00:22:58,322 --> 00:23:00,790
So I think that's just an
important tension to highlight,

526
00:23:00,790 --> 00:23:05,790
that compression is maybe
useful for extracting

527
00:23:05,790 --> 00:23:07,350
certain kinds of
structure and making

528
00:23:07,350 --> 00:23:09,190
it more readily available.

529
00:23:09,190 --> 00:23:12,870
At the same time,
if it's lossy, it

530
00:23:12,870 --> 00:23:16,310
loses information that
might be important

531
00:23:16,310 --> 00:23:21,510
and that could be useful in
some situations to some extent.

532
00:23:21,510 --> 00:23:23,070
Awesome.

533
00:23:23,070 --> 00:23:24,230
Cool.

534
00:23:24,230 --> 00:23:26,510
So the question
we're going to ask

535
00:23:26,510 --> 00:23:28,700
in the next section of the
talk is, well, all right.

536
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:30,950
So maybe statistical structures
could allow the models

537
00:23:30,950 --> 00:23:32,990
to generalize in some cases.

538
00:23:32,990 --> 00:23:35,070
But are there some
ways that we could

539
00:23:35,070 --> 00:23:37,150
get the models to
generalize, even when there

540
00:23:37,150 --> 00:23:38,730
aren't such statistical cues?

541
00:23:38,730 --> 00:23:40,230
Is there some way
that we can really

542
00:23:40,230 --> 00:23:42,830
get the models to learn
the latent structure

543
00:23:42,830 --> 00:23:44,090
or to generalize it?

544
00:23:44,090 --> 00:23:47,030

545
00:23:47,030 --> 00:23:49,450
Cool.

546
00:23:49,450 --> 00:23:53,150
And I'm going to suggest 2 and
1/2 or three paths to doing

547
00:23:53,150 --> 00:23:54,150
this.

548
00:23:54,150 --> 00:23:58,190
So the first is a train
time offline method,

549
00:23:58,190 --> 00:24:00,510
which is just that we're
going to augment the training

550
00:24:00,510 --> 00:24:04,070
data using in-context learning,
and then distill that back

551
00:24:04,070 --> 00:24:07,390
into the model's
parametric knowledge.

552
00:24:07,390 --> 00:24:11,510
And the second is one
to two online methods,

553
00:24:11,510 --> 00:24:13,750
which is that we're
going to try to retrieve

554
00:24:13,750 --> 00:24:16,190
the information from
memory at test time

555
00:24:16,190 --> 00:24:18,287
to make it into an
in-context task.

556
00:24:18,287 --> 00:24:19,870
And in particular,
we're going to look

557
00:24:19,870 --> 00:24:22,950
at both doing explicit
retrieval from episodic memory

558
00:24:22,950 --> 00:24:26,090
or implicit retrieval
that's learned via RL.

559
00:24:26,090 --> 00:24:29,740

560
00:24:29,740 --> 00:24:33,240
So the first method
is very simple.

561
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:36,300
It's the dumb first
thing we thought of.

562
00:24:36,300 --> 00:24:38,200
And it's a pretty simple idea.

563
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:41,100
It's just if in-context
learning generalizes better,

564
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:44,220
let's see if we can use it to
make those generalizations ahead

565
00:24:44,220 --> 00:24:46,740
of time and cache
them for the future.

566
00:24:46,740 --> 00:24:49,320
So what we did is we took
the data set, the model,

567
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:51,420
we prompted it with
the data set in context

568
00:24:51,420 --> 00:24:53,900
to generate
reasoning traces that

569
00:24:53,900 --> 00:24:58,200
elaborate upon the documents
that fill in missing reversals

570
00:24:58,200 --> 00:25:00,260
or make links between
documents that aren't

571
00:25:00,260 --> 00:25:02,020
explicit in the data set.

572
00:25:02,020 --> 00:25:04,820
Then we add these new
conclusions back into the data

573
00:25:04,820 --> 00:25:06,940
set and fine-tune on
this whole larger data

574
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:09,500
set, the original training data
and the augmented reasoning

575
00:25:09,500 --> 00:25:11,380
traces.

576
00:25:11,380 --> 00:25:14,720
So just to make that really
explicit, take the training set.

577
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:16,600
We concatenate it as context.

578
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:19,540
We take individual documents one
at a time and we pull them out

579
00:25:19,540 --> 00:25:22,300
and we ask the model things
like make connections

580
00:25:22,300 --> 00:25:25,580
between this document and the
other documents in context.

581
00:25:25,580 --> 00:25:27,460
And then the model
says something like,

582
00:25:27,460 --> 00:25:30,080
oh, I see that in this
document, Dax are a type of fep

583
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:31,980
and I learned earlier
or in another document

584
00:25:31,980 --> 00:25:36,700
that feps can zarp, and
therefore, Dax can zarp.

585
00:25:36,700 --> 00:25:38,980
Make sense?

586
00:25:38,980 --> 00:25:40,080
Cool.

587
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:43,780
And what we find is that this
augmented fine-tuning strategy

588
00:25:43,780 --> 00:25:46,300
can generalize, as
well as in-context

589
00:25:46,300 --> 00:25:49,740
learning on reversal-like tasks,
and maybe even better on things

590
00:25:49,740 --> 00:25:52,020
like syllogisms because
it allows the model

591
00:25:52,020 --> 00:25:56,820
more chances to fill
in missing links.

592
00:25:56,820 --> 00:25:58,940
Makes sense?

593
00:25:58,940 --> 00:26:00,000
Cool.

594
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:04,060
So one way to work around these
latent generalization failures

595
00:26:04,060 --> 00:26:08,100
is using in-context learning to
augment the fine-tuning data.

596
00:26:08,100 --> 00:26:10,360
And that can improve the
model's generalization.

597
00:26:10,360 --> 00:26:14,140

598
00:26:14,140 --> 00:26:16,940
Now, I think there's
been a kind of reaction

599
00:26:16,940 --> 00:26:19,840
against the idea of synthetic
data discovering new knowledge.

600
00:26:19,840 --> 00:26:22,500
And I hate to throw my colleague
Dave Pfau under the bus

601
00:26:22,500 --> 00:26:25,020
because he's a very
smart guy, but I

602
00:26:25,020 --> 00:26:27,930
liked to take this
screenshot from him,

603
00:26:27,930 --> 00:26:31,010
which says I'm absolutely
begging researchers to learn

604
00:26:31,010 --> 00:26:32,387
the data processing inequality.

605
00:26:32,387 --> 00:26:34,970
You can't discover new knowledge
from synthetic data generated

606
00:26:34,970 --> 00:26:37,130
from existing knowledge.

607
00:26:37,130 --> 00:26:41,770
And I think this tweet is not
wrong, but it's misleading.

608
00:26:41,770 --> 00:26:43,690
And the point I want
to make is that what

609
00:26:43,690 --> 00:26:45,650
we're doing when we
augment training data

610
00:26:45,650 --> 00:26:47,630
is not that we're
creating new information.

611
00:26:47,630 --> 00:26:50,530
The information is really
there in the data already.

612
00:26:50,530 --> 00:26:52,950
It's just that we're taking
information that's implicit,

613
00:26:52,950 --> 00:26:54,730
that's latent in
the data, and we're

614
00:26:54,730 --> 00:26:57,210
extracting it out and making
it explicit and easily

615
00:26:57,210 --> 00:26:58,730
accessible to the model.

616
00:26:58,730 --> 00:27:01,590
So, for example, if there's
a statement like all X are Y,

617
00:27:01,590 --> 00:27:04,370
no Y are Z in the
training corpus,

618
00:27:04,370 --> 00:27:08,730
then the statement no X are Z
is already implied by those.

619
00:27:08,730 --> 00:27:10,030
The information is in there.

620
00:27:10,030 --> 00:27:11,330
But it's not explicit.

621
00:27:11,330 --> 00:27:13,310
And when we do the
in-context augmentation,

622
00:27:13,310 --> 00:27:17,650
we're taking the information out
and we're making it explicit.

623
00:27:17,650 --> 00:27:19,790
And if you're interested
in this line of thinking,

624
00:27:19,790 --> 00:27:21,873
I think these are two very
nice and very different

625
00:27:21,873 --> 00:27:22,830
papers on the topic.

626
00:27:22,830 --> 00:27:25,330
One is this paper
by Tania Lombrozo,

627
00:27:25,330 --> 00:27:27,670
Learning by Thinking in
Natural and Artificial Minds,

628
00:27:27,670 --> 00:27:29,430
and this older paper
from here at Stanford

629
00:27:29,430 --> 00:27:32,530
from Stefano Ermon's group, A
Theory of Usable Information

630
00:27:32,530 --> 00:27:34,230
Under Computational Constraints.

631
00:27:34,230 --> 00:27:38,130

632
00:27:38,130 --> 00:27:41,710
OK, so we were excited
about this for a while

633
00:27:41,710 --> 00:27:44,610
and we put out a
preprint on this topic.

634
00:27:44,610 --> 00:27:47,210
But the thing that
bothered us about it

635
00:27:47,210 --> 00:27:50,370
was that it's just
not conceivable

636
00:27:50,370 --> 00:27:53,170
that we could always know
how information would

637
00:27:53,170 --> 00:27:56,250
be useful in the future in
order to augment the data ahead

638
00:27:56,250 --> 00:27:56,970
of time.

639
00:27:56,970 --> 00:27:59,610
And here's an example that I
was thinking about at the time.

640
00:27:59,610 --> 00:28:01,250
Imagine you're reading
a paper or maybe you're

641
00:28:01,250 --> 00:28:02,458
working on your dissertation.

642
00:28:02,458 --> 00:28:05,210
Here's a paper I read was
working on my dissertation.

643
00:28:05,210 --> 00:28:09,330
And it's conceivable that I
could augment my understanding

644
00:28:09,330 --> 00:28:10,850
of the paper by
thinking about how

645
00:28:10,850 --> 00:28:13,730
it could relate to my
dissertation or something.

646
00:28:13,730 --> 00:28:17,065
But it's pretty unlikely that I
would think, oh, in five years,

647
00:28:17,065 --> 00:28:19,690
I'm going to be researching how
language models use information

648
00:28:19,690 --> 00:28:22,210
in context and maybe
something from this paper

649
00:28:22,210 --> 00:28:24,228
will be relevant to my
understanding of that.

650
00:28:24,228 --> 00:28:26,770
I wasn't even really thinking
about language models that much

651
00:28:26,770 --> 00:28:29,490
at the time.

652
00:28:29,490 --> 00:28:32,170
And so the question
is how could we

653
00:28:32,170 --> 00:28:34,330
get models to use
information even

654
00:28:34,330 --> 00:28:38,850
if we didn't know at training
time that it would be useful?

655
00:28:38,850 --> 00:28:40,330
And the answer that
we came up with

656
00:28:40,330 --> 00:28:44,450
was, well, maybe this is
what retrieval is good for.

657
00:28:44,450 --> 00:28:47,770
Maybe if we can just take
knowledge from episodic memory--

658
00:28:47,770 --> 00:28:49,270
this is the
hippocampus, by the way,

659
00:28:49,270 --> 00:28:53,530
which is what does episodic
memory in humans mostly.

660
00:28:53,530 --> 00:28:55,990
We could take the knowledge
from episodic memory.

661
00:28:55,990 --> 00:28:58,090
We could retrieve
it into context.

662
00:28:58,090 --> 00:28:59,770
And then the models
would be able to use

663
00:28:59,770 --> 00:29:02,910
that knowledge more flexibly in
order to answer some question.

664
00:29:02,910 --> 00:29:06,290

665
00:29:06,290 --> 00:29:09,650
I want to highlight that,
for the experiments that I'm

666
00:29:09,650 --> 00:29:13,010
about to show you, we used an
Oracle episodic memory, which

667
00:29:13,010 --> 00:29:14,290
is totally cheating.

668
00:29:14,290 --> 00:29:18,090
And the reason we did that, it
has perfect recall but imperfect

669
00:29:18,090 --> 00:29:18,670
precision.

670
00:29:18,670 --> 00:29:20,170
So it always recalls
something that

671
00:29:20,170 --> 00:29:22,650
is useful for solving the task
but a bunch of other things

672
00:29:22,650 --> 00:29:23,700
that are distractors.

673
00:29:23,700 --> 00:29:25,700
The reason we did that
is just because we didn't

674
00:29:25,700 --> 00:29:27,400
know how to do good retrieval.

675
00:29:27,400 --> 00:29:31,040
And I'll come back to that
at the end of the talk.

676
00:29:31,040 --> 00:29:34,160
So what we find is that if we
use this cheating retrieval

677
00:29:34,160 --> 00:29:37,420
system, if we compare
on a reversal task

678
00:29:37,420 --> 00:29:40,760
the generalizations of
the forward direction,

679
00:29:40,760 --> 00:29:43,440
both parametric learning
and episodic retrieval

680
00:29:43,440 --> 00:29:46,240
do equally well
on generalization.

681
00:29:46,240 --> 00:29:48,400
But when we go to
the reversal test,

682
00:29:48,400 --> 00:29:52,100
again, parametric learning
totally fails the reversals.

683
00:29:52,100 --> 00:29:54,680
But a system that can do
episodic retrieval, even

684
00:29:54,680 --> 00:29:56,820
when it's retrieving
a bunch of distractors

685
00:29:56,820 --> 00:29:59,560
generalizes very well.

686
00:29:59,560 --> 00:30:04,880
And similarly, on the codebooks
task that I showed you earlier,

687
00:30:04,880 --> 00:30:09,240
both the parametric learner and
the system with episodic memory

688
00:30:09,240 --> 00:30:11,920
can do very well on
the training splits.

689
00:30:11,920 --> 00:30:14,640
But on the latent
test condition,

690
00:30:14,640 --> 00:30:19,080
only the system with episodic
memory can generalize well.

691
00:30:19,080 --> 00:30:25,000
So this shows some cases where
episodic retrieval, by bringing

692
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:26,740
learning experiences
into context,

693
00:30:26,740 --> 00:30:29,560
can allow the system to use
them more flexibly in order

694
00:30:29,560 --> 00:30:31,460
to support flexible
generalization.

695
00:30:31,460 --> 00:30:31,960
Yes.

696
00:30:31,960 --> 00:30:35,520
It's loading the episodes
from the [INAUDIBLE]?

697
00:30:35,520 --> 00:30:36,220
That's correct.

698
00:30:36,220 --> 00:30:39,680
Yeah, so this is just taking
documents from pre-training

699
00:30:39,680 --> 00:30:41,960
and sticking them
back into context.

700
00:30:41,960 --> 00:30:45,400
And that can dramatically
improve the generalization.

701
00:30:45,400 --> 00:30:50,480
So it can still do that X to Z
relationship that's missing just

702
00:30:50,480 --> 00:30:54,115
by pulling out X to Y
and Y to Z. In context,

703
00:30:54,115 --> 00:30:55,740
is that [INAUDIBLE]
asked the question?

704
00:30:55,740 --> 00:30:57,520
Yeah.

705
00:30:57,520 --> 00:31:00,200
Just to be clear, there's
been a long series of work

706
00:31:00,200 --> 00:31:05,040
before we worked on this topic
showing that retrieval augmented

707
00:31:05,040 --> 00:31:07,560
generation is a good
way of getting models

708
00:31:07,560 --> 00:31:10,302
to answer questions about
factual information and stuff.

709
00:31:10,302 --> 00:31:11,760
And the way I think
about this work

710
00:31:11,760 --> 00:31:14,040
is we're basically
showing a mechanism

711
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:15,220
by which this might be true.

712
00:31:15,220 --> 00:31:16,928
Even for documents
that have been trained

713
00:31:16,928 --> 00:31:20,440
into the parameters, having that
information available in context

714
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:22,590
can allow the models
to use it more flexibly

715
00:31:22,590 --> 00:31:24,050
and to answer questions better.

716
00:31:24,050 --> 00:31:27,430
Would it be fair to
say you can extract

717
00:31:27,430 --> 00:31:29,430
the true content from
pre-training quite

718
00:31:29,430 --> 00:31:32,750
well, but just not relations?

719
00:31:32,750 --> 00:31:35,050
So it's complicated.

720
00:31:35,050 --> 00:31:39,230
It's that the models can extract
the relations from pre-training

721
00:31:39,230 --> 00:31:42,870
quite well, but they need
to be cued in the right way.

722
00:31:42,870 --> 00:31:45,210
So if you ask them about it
in the forward direction,

723
00:31:45,210 --> 00:31:48,370
you can ask them in a way that's
quite different from training.

724
00:31:48,370 --> 00:31:51,090
As long as you cue it with the
first part of the relation,

725
00:31:51,090 --> 00:31:53,270
the model will do a
decent job of continuing

726
00:31:53,270 --> 00:31:55,750
with the second part.

727
00:31:55,750 --> 00:31:58,150
But it's just that
they don't have

728
00:31:58,150 --> 00:32:01,430
a good way of going backwards,
unless that information is

729
00:32:01,430 --> 00:32:02,610
already in context.

730
00:32:02,610 --> 00:32:04,950
But hold that thought because
it relates to something

731
00:32:04,950 --> 00:32:07,630
we'll come to in a second.

732
00:32:07,630 --> 00:32:08,570
Other questions?

733
00:32:08,570 --> 00:32:13,390

734
00:32:13,390 --> 00:32:18,630
OK, so we totally cheated on the
episodic retrieval part there.

735
00:32:18,630 --> 00:32:22,630
But a natural question you
can ask, as I was alluding to,

736
00:32:22,630 --> 00:32:24,390
is whether the
models could learn

737
00:32:24,390 --> 00:32:26,950
how to do this via
reinforcement learning

738
00:32:26,950 --> 00:32:32,150
without needing to do explicit
retrieval or augmentation.

739
00:32:32,150 --> 00:32:34,750
And the intuition
here is that-- look

740
00:32:34,750 --> 00:32:36,370
at this graph I
showed you earlier.

741
00:32:36,370 --> 00:32:38,610
The models already
know the relations.

742
00:32:38,610 --> 00:32:40,890
They can state them
in a new context.

743
00:32:40,890 --> 00:32:42,470
They already know
if the relation

744
00:32:42,470 --> 00:32:44,990
is in context how to
do the reversal using

745
00:32:44,990 --> 00:32:46,550
in-context learning.

746
00:32:46,550 --> 00:32:49,110
They just need to put
those pieces together

747
00:32:49,110 --> 00:32:50,730
in order to solve the task.

748
00:32:50,730 --> 00:32:54,970
And so what we want to
ask in this paper is,

749
00:32:54,970 --> 00:32:57,510
could the model learn to
regenerate the information

750
00:32:57,510 --> 00:33:01,030
that it needs in its
chain of thought,

751
00:33:01,030 --> 00:33:03,710
thereby bringing that
information back into context

752
00:33:03,710 --> 00:33:06,183
and enabling it to
use it more flexibly?

753
00:33:06,183 --> 00:33:08,350
And in particular, can we
use reinforcement learning

754
00:33:08,350 --> 00:33:09,890
to teach the models to do this?

755
00:33:09,890 --> 00:33:13,190

756
00:33:13,190 --> 00:33:17,830
So the way we tested this is we
took a model, we fine-tuned it

757
00:33:17,830 --> 00:33:20,990
on several data sets
containing new information,

758
00:33:20,990 --> 00:33:23,710
completely non-overlapping
information, even though I copy

759
00:33:23,710 --> 00:33:27,590
pasted these documents here so
it looks like it's overlapping.

760
00:33:27,590 --> 00:33:30,350
And then, of course, we can
test the model's generalization

761
00:33:30,350 --> 00:33:33,830
just after fine-tuning, as
we did in the earlier papers.

762
00:33:33,830 --> 00:33:36,630
But we can also do
reinforcement learning

763
00:33:36,630 --> 00:33:40,470
to teach the model how to
reason on one of these data sets

764
00:33:40,470 --> 00:33:42,830
and then test whether
that allows the model

765
00:33:42,830 --> 00:33:46,350
to learn how to reason
to generalize better

766
00:33:46,350 --> 00:33:48,390
on the other data set.

767
00:33:48,390 --> 00:33:51,630
Does that make sense?

768
00:33:51,630 --> 00:33:54,070
Awesome.

769
00:33:54,070 --> 00:33:56,970
And what we find is
that doing this process,

770
00:33:56,970 --> 00:34:01,230
if we do reinforcement
learning on--

771
00:34:01,230 --> 00:34:02,990
whoops.

772
00:34:02,990 --> 00:34:05,950
Some editing issue here.

773
00:34:05,950 --> 00:34:09,050
If we do reinforcement
learning on one data set,

774
00:34:09,050 --> 00:34:14,550
data set A, it
generalizes to data set--

775
00:34:14,550 --> 00:34:18,260
to reasoning on
data set B. Whereas,

776
00:34:18,260 --> 00:34:23,139
this augmentation bar here is if
you just do augmentation on data

777
00:34:23,139 --> 00:34:24,139
set E--

778
00:34:24,139 --> 00:34:30,500
sorry, data set A, it doesn't
generalize to data set B.

779
00:34:30,500 --> 00:34:33,540
This is showing the
generalization for syllogisms.

780
00:34:33,540 --> 00:34:35,520
You can see the amount
of generalization.

781
00:34:35,520 --> 00:34:39,300
This red bar is the
reasoning generalization.

782
00:34:39,300 --> 00:34:41,139
You can see the amount
of generalization

783
00:34:41,139 --> 00:34:44,300
varies across conditions.

784
00:34:44,300 --> 00:34:47,540
And in particular,
reversals don't

785
00:34:47,540 --> 00:34:50,100
get that much
generalization benefit

786
00:34:50,100 --> 00:34:54,100
from this in-context
recall strategy as opposed

787
00:34:54,100 --> 00:34:56,900
to retrieval which
did very well.

788
00:34:56,900 --> 00:35:01,140
And the reason this
is is that, basically,

789
00:35:01,140 --> 00:35:04,020
in order to solve a
reversal-type problem,

790
00:35:04,020 --> 00:35:05,860
the model has to
recall the information

791
00:35:05,860 --> 00:35:06,920
in the other direction.

792
00:35:06,920 --> 00:35:10,060
And the whole point of
reversal is that that's hard.

793
00:35:10,060 --> 00:35:13,260
It's hard to get from one
direction to the other.

794
00:35:13,260 --> 00:35:15,340
And so the way the
model is able to get

795
00:35:15,340 --> 00:35:17,460
anything above chance
on this is just

796
00:35:17,460 --> 00:35:20,300
going through and enumerating
all the things it's

797
00:35:20,300 --> 00:35:24,040
seen in the data set until it
happens to hit the right one,

798
00:35:24,040 --> 00:35:26,940
and then it can go, oh,
I can reverse that now.

799
00:35:26,940 --> 00:35:29,620
And so the model is able
to do this above chance

800
00:35:29,620 --> 00:35:32,780
on the test set because
the test set only

801
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:34,720
has thousands of entities.

802
00:35:34,720 --> 00:35:36,620
And so a decent
amount of the time, it

803
00:35:36,620 --> 00:35:38,340
can enumerate hundreds
of them and happen

804
00:35:38,340 --> 00:35:39,640
to hit on the right one.

805
00:35:39,640 --> 00:35:43,280
But obviously, this isn't a very
practical solution at scale.

806
00:35:43,280 --> 00:35:46,140

807
00:35:46,140 --> 00:35:51,140
So this shows a glass
half full story.

808
00:35:51,140 --> 00:35:53,460
Models can learn
via reinforcement

809
00:35:53,460 --> 00:35:55,900
learning how to regenerate
information that they

810
00:35:55,900 --> 00:35:59,020
need in context at test time.

811
00:35:59,020 --> 00:36:01,940
And this can help with some
of the problems quite a lot,

812
00:36:01,940 --> 00:36:05,900
but doesn't necessarily
help with all of them.

813
00:36:05,900 --> 00:36:06,540
Yes.

814
00:36:06,540 --> 00:36:08,700
[INAUDIBLE]

815
00:36:08,700 --> 00:36:12,100
The augmentation.

816
00:36:12,100 --> 00:36:16,950
So this is different because the
augmentations was at train time.

817
00:36:16,950 --> 00:36:18,630
So when you have
the documents there,

818
00:36:18,630 --> 00:36:21,003
then you generate
thinking traces from them.

819
00:36:21,003 --> 00:36:23,170
And that's an easier problem
because the information

820
00:36:23,170 --> 00:36:24,650
is available.

821
00:36:24,650 --> 00:36:27,650
This is the model has to learn
how to pull the information out

822
00:36:27,650 --> 00:36:30,450
that's relevant
to solving a task.

823
00:36:30,450 --> 00:36:32,313
And for something
like syllogisms,

824
00:36:32,313 --> 00:36:33,730
it's able to do
that decently well

825
00:36:33,730 --> 00:36:35,790
because you can follow
the chain of links.

826
00:36:35,790 --> 00:36:37,930
You can be like, oh, let's see.

827
00:36:37,930 --> 00:36:41,650
I know all Dax are--

828
00:36:41,650 --> 00:36:43,350
what do I know about Dax?

829
00:36:43,350 --> 00:36:45,490
So let's see all Dax
are fep or whatever.

830
00:36:45,490 --> 00:36:47,930
And so it can reason
through the chain

831
00:36:47,930 --> 00:36:49,710
to solve those
kinds of problems.

832
00:36:49,710 --> 00:36:52,050
For reversals, it's harder.

833
00:36:52,050 --> 00:36:55,730
But reversals are very easy
with in-context augmentation,

834
00:36:55,730 --> 00:36:59,222
especially because at least
reversals you can anticipate

835
00:36:59,222 --> 00:37:01,430
might be something you would
care about at test time.

836
00:37:01,430 --> 00:37:05,170

837
00:37:05,170 --> 00:37:06,190
Cool.

838
00:37:06,190 --> 00:37:08,450
So I've shown you a
few different paths

839
00:37:08,450 --> 00:37:13,730
to better generalization from
leveraging in-context reasoning.

840
00:37:13,730 --> 00:37:17,410
The first is a train
time or offline path,

841
00:37:17,410 --> 00:37:19,570
where we augment the
training data in context

842
00:37:19,570 --> 00:37:22,290
and then distill those
augmented data into the model's

843
00:37:22,290 --> 00:37:23,770
parametric knowledge.

844
00:37:23,770 --> 00:37:27,010
And the second are some
online test time paths

845
00:37:27,010 --> 00:37:29,770
where we either
explicitly retrieve memory

846
00:37:29,770 --> 00:37:33,010
or we train the model via IRL to
implicitly retrieve information

847
00:37:33,010 --> 00:37:34,370
from the training corpus.

848
00:37:34,370 --> 00:37:36,530
And either way, having
that information available

849
00:37:36,530 --> 00:37:39,930
back in context makes the models
better at answering these test

850
00:37:39,930 --> 00:37:41,050
questions.

851
00:37:41,050 --> 00:37:42,130
Yes.

852
00:37:42,130 --> 00:37:43,930
What's the difference
between the models

853
00:37:43,930 --> 00:37:46,910
being able to do this,
generate this augmented data,

854
00:37:46,910 --> 00:37:50,930
and then also do
[INAUDIBLE] regeneration

855
00:37:50,930 --> 00:37:54,770
and just doing it [INAUDIBLE]?

856
00:37:54,770 --> 00:37:58,570
So it's basically--
well, actually,

857
00:37:58,570 --> 00:38:01,530
let me go to the next slide.

858
00:38:01,530 --> 00:38:04,310
And maybe this
will make it clear.

859
00:38:04,310 --> 00:38:05,890
And if not, we can
come back to it.

860
00:38:05,890 --> 00:38:10,010
So basically, these methods
have different trade-offs.

861
00:38:10,010 --> 00:38:12,490
So first of all, let's just
talk about the performance.

862
00:38:12,490 --> 00:38:14,155
Doing train time augmentation.

863
00:38:14,155 --> 00:38:15,530
If you know what
questions you're

864
00:38:15,530 --> 00:38:17,310
going to care
about at test time,

865
00:38:17,310 --> 00:38:19,490
this can achieve performance
as good or better

866
00:38:19,490 --> 00:38:21,090
than in-context learning.

867
00:38:21,090 --> 00:38:23,430
If we do test time
episodic retrieval,

868
00:38:23,430 --> 00:38:25,330
this can also achieve
good performance.

869
00:38:25,330 --> 00:38:27,890
And this RL-based method
achieves good performance

870
00:38:27,890 --> 00:38:31,130
on many kinds of structures,
but not all of them.

871
00:38:31,130 --> 00:38:33,610
So the differences
between these methods

872
00:38:33,610 --> 00:38:37,170
are really about
what they cost when.

873
00:38:37,170 --> 00:38:40,110
And so for the train
time augmentation method,

874
00:38:40,110 --> 00:38:42,950
you have to spend a lot of
extra compute at training time.

875
00:38:42,950 --> 00:38:46,410
You take a bunch of
long context inferences

876
00:38:46,410 --> 00:38:47,970
where you take a
data set and then

877
00:38:47,970 --> 00:38:51,890
you generate further
information from it.

878
00:38:51,890 --> 00:38:58,187
So that's relatively expensive
compute-wise at training time.

879
00:38:58,187 --> 00:38:59,770
But it's relatively
easy for the model

880
00:38:59,770 --> 00:39:01,437
because it has the
information available

881
00:39:01,437 --> 00:39:04,570
and it just has to
generate other inferences

882
00:39:04,570 --> 00:39:05,862
it can make from it.

883
00:39:05,862 --> 00:39:08,070
And it's efficient at test
time because at test time,

884
00:39:08,070 --> 00:39:09,987
we don't do anything
different than if we just

885
00:39:09,987 --> 00:39:11,280
fine tune the model normally.

886
00:39:11,280 --> 00:39:14,480
So it's expensive at
training, free at test.

887
00:39:14,480 --> 00:39:16,480
Doing test time
retrieval, well, it's

888
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:20,360
free at training
time, which is nice,

889
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:23,580
but it requires a bunch of extra
inference compute at test time

890
00:39:23,580 --> 00:39:25,880
because now we're making
the test context longer

891
00:39:25,880 --> 00:39:28,240
by prepending a bunch
of documents to them.

892
00:39:28,240 --> 00:39:29,295
That's more expensive.

893
00:39:29,295 --> 00:39:30,920
And I didn't give
you an answer for how

894
00:39:30,920 --> 00:39:32,860
to do retrieval while
in a general way.

895
00:39:32,860 --> 00:39:34,660
So this isn't even
really a solution.

896
00:39:34,660 --> 00:39:37,680
It's just a gesture at
where a solution might be.

897
00:39:37,680 --> 00:39:43,840
This RL method, it
generalizes beyond things

898
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:45,280
that we anticipated
ahead of time

899
00:39:45,280 --> 00:39:46,960
we would need to
augment for along

900
00:39:46,960 --> 00:39:49,080
with this one, which is nice.

901
00:39:49,080 --> 00:39:51,298
But it requires extra
inference compute

902
00:39:51,298 --> 00:39:53,840
at training time when you're
doing the reinforcement learning

903
00:39:53,840 --> 00:39:56,840
step and at test time, when the
model has to generate a longer

904
00:39:56,840 --> 00:39:59,280
thinking trace in order
to pull things back out

905
00:39:59,280 --> 00:40:01,320
of its knowledge.

906
00:40:01,320 --> 00:40:05,120
And so, I would say, to
come back to your question,

907
00:40:05,120 --> 00:40:08,800
this one is, in
some sense-- well,

908
00:40:08,800 --> 00:40:11,440
these two are in some
sense easier for the model

909
00:40:11,440 --> 00:40:14,300
because the information gets
into its context somehow.

910
00:40:14,300 --> 00:40:17,720
And all it does is have to
use it in the right way.

911
00:40:17,720 --> 00:40:19,760
Test time retrieval is
maybe the easiest of all

912
00:40:19,760 --> 00:40:23,560
because it knows what question
it's going to use it for.

913
00:40:23,560 --> 00:40:25,120
Train time augmentation
is the model

914
00:40:25,120 --> 00:40:28,280
has to fill in what are the
ways this information might

915
00:40:28,280 --> 00:40:30,480
be useful in the future?

916
00:40:30,480 --> 00:40:33,440
Test time thinking
is hard for the model

917
00:40:33,440 --> 00:40:36,440
because although it knows what
question it's trying to answer,

918
00:40:36,440 --> 00:40:39,160
it doesn't necessarily
know which information

919
00:40:39,160 --> 00:40:40,660
might be useful for that.

920
00:40:40,660 --> 00:40:43,020
And so at least for
things like reversals,

921
00:40:43,020 --> 00:40:45,280
it might have to go through
a lot of information

922
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:48,480
to try to pull out something
that would be useful.

923
00:40:48,480 --> 00:40:50,840
Make sense?

924
00:40:50,840 --> 00:40:53,000
Awesome.

925
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:54,080
Cool.

926
00:40:54,080 --> 00:40:59,400
So in the last 10 minutes or so
of the talk, what I want to do

927
00:40:59,400 --> 00:41:01,040
is talk a bit about
the bigger picture

928
00:41:01,040 --> 00:41:02,700
of why I've been
interested in these things

929
00:41:02,700 --> 00:41:04,367
and how it might
relate to some findings

930
00:41:04,367 --> 00:41:08,080
in natural intelligence.

931
00:41:08,080 --> 00:41:10,920
So what I told you at
the beginning of the talk

932
00:41:10,920 --> 00:41:14,440
is that training data
often latently convey more

933
00:41:14,440 --> 00:41:16,880
than their explicit content.

934
00:41:16,880 --> 00:41:19,560
And when you train a
language model's parameters

935
00:41:19,560 --> 00:41:23,560
on these data, what it learns
might be overly tied to what

936
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:25,500
was explicitly conveyed there.

937
00:41:25,500 --> 00:41:28,640

938
00:41:28,640 --> 00:41:31,760
And so the question that I got
interested in while we were

939
00:41:31,760 --> 00:41:33,680
working on this
is is it possible

940
00:41:33,680 --> 00:41:37,160
that natural intelligence faces
some of these same challenges,

941
00:41:37,160 --> 00:41:40,400
like the parametric
learning that we do

942
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:42,600
might be more tied
to the explicit way

943
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:46,440
that we've learned
some information.

944
00:41:46,440 --> 00:41:49,240
And if so, there
might be evidence

945
00:41:49,240 --> 00:41:51,640
that natural
intelligence also uses

946
00:41:51,640 --> 00:41:53,120
these different
kinds of strategies

947
00:41:53,120 --> 00:41:55,200
to bridge the
generalization gap,

948
00:41:55,200 --> 00:41:59,640
doing things like augmenting
or training experiences offline

949
00:41:59,640 --> 00:42:03,640
and retrieving relevant
information online.

950
00:42:03,640 --> 00:42:05,120
And it turns out
that if you look

951
00:42:05,120 --> 00:42:07,120
at the literature
on the relationship

952
00:42:07,120 --> 00:42:08,870
between the
hippocampus, which does

953
00:42:08,870 --> 00:42:11,870
episodic memory in
the brain, roughly,

954
00:42:11,870 --> 00:42:16,650
and the rest of neocortex, which
does more parametric learning,

955
00:42:16,650 --> 00:42:20,230
roughly, you'll find that
the relationship between them

956
00:42:20,230 --> 00:42:22,030
is pretty complicated.

957
00:42:22,030 --> 00:42:24,870
As this paper says, it's not a
simple function of experience

958
00:42:24,870 --> 00:42:26,710
with the hippocampus replays.

959
00:42:26,710 --> 00:42:29,110
And you can find evidence
that the hippocampus

960
00:42:29,110 --> 00:42:33,430
is doing things that look
like offline augmentation.

961
00:42:33,430 --> 00:42:37,310
It's doing preemptive
replay of future problems

962
00:42:37,310 --> 00:42:40,870
that might come up or
that it is reorganizing

963
00:42:40,870 --> 00:42:44,710
our experiences in ways that
might support generalization.

964
00:42:44,710 --> 00:42:49,270
And you can find some evidence
that online replay is helping

965
00:42:49,270 --> 00:42:50,950
us to solve
problems, or at least

966
00:42:50,950 --> 00:42:53,870
could be helping us
to solve problems.

967
00:42:53,870 --> 00:42:57,070
And so it's possible
that natural intelligence

968
00:42:57,070 --> 00:42:59,430
takes a let's throw
everything at the wall

969
00:42:59,430 --> 00:43:01,870
and see what sticks approach
to solving these latent

970
00:43:01,870 --> 00:43:08,990
generalization problems by using
both offline and online methods.

971
00:43:08,990 --> 00:43:11,650
And this, if you're interested
in the neuroscience of it,

972
00:43:11,650 --> 00:43:14,150
could provide a
interesting and different,

973
00:43:14,150 --> 00:43:16,350
I think, from other
perspectives perspective

974
00:43:16,350 --> 00:43:19,670
on why cortical and hippocampal
learning are complementary,

975
00:43:19,670 --> 00:43:22,510
where, again, the parametric
learning is able to integrate

976
00:43:22,510 --> 00:43:25,050
information, statistical
structures, for example,

977
00:43:25,050 --> 00:43:26,830
across many experiences.

978
00:43:26,830 --> 00:43:29,950
But episodic memory, by
preserving specific experiences

979
00:43:29,950 --> 00:43:32,150
in richer detail,
makes them more

980
00:43:32,150 --> 00:43:34,470
available for
flexible reuse later

981
00:43:34,470 --> 00:43:36,610
to solve different
kinds of problems.

982
00:43:36,610 --> 00:43:39,790

983
00:43:39,790 --> 00:43:40,415
Cool.

984
00:43:40,415 --> 00:43:41,790
So coming back to
the big picture

985
00:43:41,790 --> 00:43:44,070
question I posed at the
beginning of the talk, what

986
00:43:44,070 --> 00:43:47,190
computational ingredients are
needed for generalization?

987
00:43:47,190 --> 00:43:48,870
Well, what I want to
suggest is that we

988
00:43:48,870 --> 00:43:52,670
need both consolidated
information, like cache

989
00:43:52,670 --> 00:43:56,190
statistics, facts, and insights
within or across experiences,

990
00:43:56,190 --> 00:44:00,470
procedures for reasoning about
information flexibly in context,

991
00:44:00,470 --> 00:44:03,430
and we need episodic memory
that preserves experiences

992
00:44:03,430 --> 00:44:07,020
in rich detail, allowing access
to information that we didn't

993
00:44:07,020 --> 00:44:10,380
realize would be
important later.

994
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:12,540
And you can think
of these things

995
00:44:12,540 --> 00:44:16,060
as being some of the functions
of parameters in the language

996
00:44:16,060 --> 00:44:17,882
models on the one
hand and the things

997
00:44:17,882 --> 00:44:20,340
that we put in context for them
on the other hand, at least

998
00:44:20,340 --> 00:44:26,620
with methods like retrieval
augmented generation.

999
00:44:26,620 --> 00:44:27,480
Cool.

1000
00:44:27,480 --> 00:44:30,660
So that's it for what
I wanted to say today.

1001
00:44:30,660 --> 00:44:32,780
I told you that training
experiences contain

1002
00:44:32,780 --> 00:44:35,220
both explicit content and
they convey latent information

1003
00:44:35,220 --> 00:44:36,570
that might be useful later.

1004
00:44:36,570 --> 00:44:38,820
Language models might not
reason over this effectively

1005
00:44:38,820 --> 00:44:39,880
from parametric learning.

1006
00:44:39,880 --> 00:44:41,980
But they can use it
flexibly in context.

1007
00:44:41,980 --> 00:44:43,580
We showed a variety
of approaches

1008
00:44:43,580 --> 00:44:46,940
to bridging this gap, offline
approaches using augmentation

1009
00:44:46,940 --> 00:44:49,962
and online using various
forms of retrieval.

1010
00:44:49,962 --> 00:44:51,420
And I've suggested
that these might

1011
00:44:51,420 --> 00:44:54,060
relate to some findings about
offline and online hippocampal

1012
00:44:54,060 --> 00:44:55,380
replay.

1013
00:44:55,380 --> 00:44:56,980
Of course, there
might be other things

1014
00:44:56,980 --> 00:44:58,980
that the brain is doing
that's smarter than what

1015
