1034
00:45:49,120 --> 00:45:57,180

1035
00:45:57,180 --> 00:45:58,860
Thanks for a good talk.

1036
00:45:58,860 --> 00:46:01,940
I was wondering, do you have any
ablation study or sensitivity

1037
00:46:01,940 --> 00:46:06,420
activity towards contact size
and billions of parameters

1038
00:46:06,420 --> 00:46:07,860
and also sparsity?

1039
00:46:07,860 --> 00:46:13,740
And how do they impact or
influence your results?

1040
00:46:13,740 --> 00:46:14,840
Yeah, great question.

1041
00:46:14,840 --> 00:46:21,260
So for context size, we
do have some experiments

1042
00:46:21,260 --> 00:46:23,240
that bear on this.

1043
00:46:23,240 --> 00:46:30,500
So I guess the short answer is
that it's quite model dependent.

1044
00:46:30,500 --> 00:46:35,940
But I can tell you that for
the models that we used,

1045
00:46:35,940 --> 00:46:40,800
they're pretty good at--

1046
00:46:40,800 --> 00:46:44,440
if you give them just one piece
of information in context,

1047
00:46:44,440 --> 00:46:45,840
they will always get it right.

1048
00:46:45,840 --> 00:46:50,680
If you give them hundreds of
thousands of tokens in context,

1049
00:46:50,680 --> 00:46:53,480
they'll get it a decent
amount of the time.

1050
00:46:53,480 --> 00:46:55,160
It depends on the problem.

1051
00:46:55,160 --> 00:46:57,843
But it also depends a lot
on what the information is.

1052
00:46:57,843 --> 00:47:00,260
And so there's actually one
of the ablations in our paper.

1053
00:47:00,260 --> 00:47:02,850
We look at the reversal
[? course ?] data set,

1054
00:47:02,850 --> 00:47:05,530
and we just change
every word in there

1055
00:47:05,530 --> 00:47:07,550
to nonsense instead of
the original things,

1056
00:47:07,550 --> 00:47:10,290
which are plausible statements
about this person directed

1057
00:47:10,290 --> 00:47:11,950
this movie.

1058
00:47:11,950 --> 00:47:13,810
And what we find
is that the model's

1059
00:47:13,810 --> 00:47:16,050
ability with the same data
set in context, but just

1060
00:47:16,050 --> 00:47:19,490
with the names, for example,
replaced with nonsense words,

1061
00:47:19,490 --> 00:47:22,470
the model's ability to answer
those questions goes down a lot.

1062
00:47:22,470 --> 00:47:26,230
Now, that might be partly a
context length changing confound

1063
00:47:26,230 --> 00:47:28,490
because the tokenization
for the names

1064
00:47:28,490 --> 00:47:31,510
is shorter than tokenization
for the nonsense words.

1065
00:47:31,510 --> 00:47:33,990
But I think it's too big an
effect to be explained by that.

1066
00:47:33,990 --> 00:47:37,710
And it's really that retrieval
is context-sensitive.

1067
00:47:37,710 --> 00:47:40,650
The model's ability to pull
information out of context

1068
00:47:40,650 --> 00:47:43,370
depends on how well it's
learned in pre-training

1069
00:47:43,370 --> 00:47:45,510
to pull information of
that type out of context.

1070
00:47:45,510 --> 00:47:48,130
And it's just better at
doing that for information

1071
00:47:48,130 --> 00:47:49,650
about real entities,
which comes up

1072
00:47:49,650 --> 00:47:52,010
all the time in pre-training
than for information

1073
00:47:52,010 --> 00:47:53,670
about nonsense words.

1074
00:47:53,670 --> 00:47:57,490
So the answer is that
the models are definitely

1075
00:47:57,490 --> 00:48:01,890
sensitive to the context
length and that sensitivity

1076
00:48:01,890 --> 00:48:05,790
depends on the information that
you're asking them to retrieve.

1077
00:48:05,790 --> 00:48:08,690

1078
00:48:08,690 --> 00:48:10,845
There was a-- oh, you
asked about sparsity too.

1079
00:48:10,845 --> 00:48:11,970
Parameters and [INAUDIBLE]?

1080
00:48:11,970 --> 00:48:13,590
Yeah, parameters.

1081
00:48:13,590 --> 00:48:15,530
So I would say, short
answer on parameters

1082
00:48:15,530 --> 00:48:20,050
is that larger models are
better at this like most things.

1083
00:48:20,050 --> 00:48:25,730
Sparsity, you mean mixture of
experts versus dense models?

1084
00:48:25,730 --> 00:48:28,390
I don't have any experiments
that directly address that.

1085
00:48:28,390 --> 00:48:29,790
So I can't answer.

1086
00:48:29,790 --> 00:48:32,990
I wouldn't expect it to
make that much difference

1087
00:48:32,990 --> 00:48:36,210
because, usually, the
sparsity of the model

1088
00:48:36,210 --> 00:48:38,670
is in the MLP layers,
not in the attention.

1089
00:48:38,670 --> 00:48:40,490
It's really the attention
that's modulating

1090
00:48:40,490 --> 00:48:43,330
these in-context effects.

1091
00:48:43,330 --> 00:48:45,970
But it could.

1092
00:48:45,970 --> 00:48:46,470
Yeah.

1093
00:48:46,470 --> 00:48:47,430
[INAUDIBLE]

1094
00:48:47,430 --> 00:48:53,690

1095
00:48:53,690 --> 00:48:59,040
Another thing is that you train
to learn new things easier.

1096
00:48:59,040 --> 00:49:00,460
But there's a tension in that.

1097
00:49:00,460 --> 00:49:04,237
I was just wondering what
assumption of sparsity

1098
00:49:04,237 --> 00:49:04,820
that you used.

1099
00:49:04,820 --> 00:49:05,320
I see.

1100
00:49:05,320 --> 00:49:08,580
Yeah, so most of the
experiments I told you about,

1101
00:49:08,580 --> 00:49:10,960
at least the small
model experiments

1102
00:49:10,960 --> 00:49:14,680
were done with dense models.

1103
00:49:14,680 --> 00:49:18,560
Yeah, it would be interesting
to compare the sparse case.

1104
00:49:18,560 --> 00:49:24,840
[INAUDIBLE] in which
closely related to RL

1105
00:49:24,840 --> 00:49:26,680
but it's not really real RL?

1106
00:49:26,680 --> 00:49:29,420
I find that sometimes when
you're having these models,

1107
00:49:29,420 --> 00:49:33,160
you work on doing feedback,
and the application [INAUDIBLE]

1108
00:49:33,160 --> 00:49:36,400
feedback is not
really demoralizing

1109
00:49:36,400 --> 00:49:41,400
in the sense coming to update
the weights while you're

1110
00:49:41,400 --> 00:49:42,400
learning.

1111
00:49:42,400 --> 00:49:49,330
And so [INAUDIBLE]
update that in real-time.

1112
00:49:49,330 --> 00:49:51,580
So, of course, I was trying
to do something like that.

1113
00:49:51,580 --> 00:49:55,000
But it's not
real-time real-time.

1114
00:49:55,000 --> 00:49:56,060
[INAUDIBLE]

1115
00:49:56,060 --> 00:49:57,960

1116
00:49:57,960 --> 00:50:03,780
Yeah, so I guess there's
several questions there.

1117
00:50:03,780 --> 00:50:09,480

1118
00:50:09,480 --> 00:50:17,923
In general, there's
a problem of-- so I'm

1119
00:50:17,923 --> 00:50:19,840
going to step back and
give a very big picture

1120
00:50:19,840 --> 00:50:22,080
answer to this question.

1121
00:50:22,080 --> 00:50:23,960
One of the things
that people thought

1122
00:50:23,960 --> 00:50:28,320
about why hippocampal and
cortical learning in humans

1123
00:50:28,320 --> 00:50:30,040
were complementary
originally, which

1124
00:50:30,040 --> 00:50:32,248
I think is still a very
important part of why they're

1125
00:50:32,248 --> 00:50:35,200
complementary, is
that it's important

1126
00:50:35,200 --> 00:50:38,420
for the parametric
learning system

1127
00:50:38,420 --> 00:50:42,080
to learn slowly because
that allows it to integrate

1128
00:50:42,080 --> 00:50:44,280
information across
a lot of experiences

1129
00:50:44,280 --> 00:50:45,660
without having interference.

1130
00:50:45,660 --> 00:50:49,880
And that's what helps the system
to do statistical generalization

1131
00:50:49,880 --> 00:50:51,600
that is useful.

1132
00:50:51,600 --> 00:50:55,560
On the other hand, you want to
be able to-- if you're out there

1133
00:50:55,560 --> 00:50:58,498
in the world and you almost get
in a car accident or something,

1134
00:50:58,498 --> 00:51:01,040
you want to be able to learn
from that one experience and not

1135
00:51:01,040 --> 00:51:04,320
have to see it 50,000 times
in order for the statistics

1136
00:51:04,320 --> 00:51:05,340
to finally catch up.

1137
00:51:05,340 --> 00:51:10,000
And so episodic memory stores
the experiences very rapidly

1138
00:51:10,000 --> 00:51:11,640
from a single experience.

1139
00:51:11,640 --> 00:51:14,220
And then by replaying
it over time,

1140
00:51:14,220 --> 00:51:16,880
it can integrate that
with your other knowledge

1141
00:51:16,880 --> 00:51:19,560
and your other
experiences in a way that

1142
00:51:19,560 --> 00:51:22,380
preserves the nice slow
learning properties of cortex

1143
00:51:22,380 --> 00:51:25,400
but gives you the benefits
of fast generalization.

1144
00:51:25,400 --> 00:51:28,520
So indeed, with
that analogy, you

1145
00:51:28,520 --> 00:51:32,440
might think that if you just try
to train the model's parameters

1146
00:51:32,440 --> 00:51:36,320
from one experience, if you
make a large enough update

1147
00:51:36,320 --> 00:51:39,600
to the model's parameters
in order to get it to really

1148
00:51:39,600 --> 00:51:44,040
effectively incorporate that
experience in a way that

1149
00:51:44,040 --> 00:51:46,603
generalizes to other
instances, you might actually

1150
00:51:46,603 --> 00:51:49,020
damage some of the model's
other knowledge in the process.

1151
00:51:49,020 --> 00:51:50,640
And so what you
need to do is maybe

1152
00:51:50,640 --> 00:51:54,280
some way of storing it
explicitly and either retrieving

1153
00:51:54,280 --> 00:51:56,710
that when you need
it or replaying it

1154
00:51:56,710 --> 00:51:59,690
on the model to get it to learn
that information over time.

1155
00:51:59,690 --> 00:52:07,270
I think people do things
like this to some extent.

1156
00:52:07,270 --> 00:52:12,430
I suspect that the issues
with model generalization

1157
00:52:12,430 --> 00:52:18,110
are more about the models
being too context-sensitive

1158
00:52:18,110 --> 00:52:19,990
in some sense in
what they learn.

1159
00:52:19,990 --> 00:52:22,630
The models are very good at
distinguishing different kinds

1160
00:52:22,630 --> 00:52:23,690
of situations.

1161
00:52:23,690 --> 00:52:25,870
And because of
that, they're very

1162
00:52:25,870 --> 00:52:29,110
good at not generalizing
as far as you want them

1163
00:52:29,110 --> 00:52:33,790
to in certain kinds of ways.

1164
00:52:33,790 --> 00:52:35,710
The model gets some
human feedback.

1165
00:52:35,710 --> 00:52:37,740
I don't like this response.

1166
00:52:37,740 --> 00:52:38,490
And it's like, OK.

1167
00:52:38,490 --> 00:52:40,050
When someone asked
me this question,

1168
00:52:40,050 --> 00:52:41,530
I won't give this response.

1169
00:52:41,530 --> 00:52:43,210
But if you ask a very
similar question,

1170
00:52:43,210 --> 00:52:44,930
it might not have
generalized to that.

1171
00:52:44,930 --> 00:52:47,930
So that's my intuition
about what's going on there.

1172
00:52:47,930 --> 00:52:54,070
But definitely, I think doing
retrieval of relevant examples

1173
00:52:54,070 --> 00:52:58,110
could help with some of
these kinds of problems.

1174
00:52:58,110 --> 00:53:00,443
It might cause other
issues, though,

1175
00:53:00,443 --> 00:53:02,110
depending on what
kind of generalization

1176
00:53:02,110 --> 00:53:04,110
you were looking for.

1177
00:53:04,110 --> 00:53:05,870
Hopefully that answered it.

1178
00:53:05,870 --> 00:53:08,350
What do you think of the
fact that the model gets

1179
00:53:08,350 --> 00:53:12,190
smaller or gets [INAUDIBLE]?

1180
00:53:12,190 --> 00:53:16,670
Maybe it will contribute to
this with generic learning

1181
00:53:16,670 --> 00:53:18,910
after, let's say, after a month.

1182
00:53:18,910 --> 00:53:22,270
You can have this network where
they say, hey, I love this.

1183
00:53:22,270 --> 00:53:23,090
[INAUDIBLE]

1184
00:53:23,090 --> 00:53:27,630

1185
00:53:27,630 --> 00:53:29,750
Yeah, I think there's
lots of interesting things

1186
00:53:29,750 --> 00:53:32,123
to be explored in that space.

1187
00:53:32,123 --> 00:53:34,230
Yeah, a question
about the slide.

1188
00:53:34,230 --> 00:53:36,650
You compared the pros and
cons of the three approaches.

1189
00:53:36,650 --> 00:53:37,150
Yes.

1190
00:53:37,150 --> 00:53:40,150
Training-data
augmentation, ICL, and RL.

1191
00:53:40,150 --> 00:53:40,687
Yeah.

1192
00:53:40,687 --> 00:53:42,270
Training data
augmentation was written

1193
00:53:42,270 --> 00:53:46,970
that it achieves as good or
better performance than ICL.

1194
00:53:46,970 --> 00:53:50,450
So I wonder in which cases it's
achieving better performance?

1195
00:53:50,450 --> 00:54:02,080
Yeah, so the basic situation
in which that happens is that--

1196
00:54:02,080 --> 00:54:04,773
so think about it this way.

1197
00:54:04,773 --> 00:54:06,940
In the in-context learning
case, the model basically

1198
00:54:06,940 --> 00:54:11,660
gets one thinking chain's
worth to try to hit

1199
00:54:11,660 --> 00:54:13,820
the answer to the question.

1200
00:54:13,820 --> 00:54:18,240
In the case that we did the
in-context augmentation,

1201
00:54:18,240 --> 00:54:20,660
we actually have
a lot of documents

1202
00:54:20,660 --> 00:54:22,740
that we ask the
model to generate

1203
00:54:22,740 --> 00:54:24,960
thinking traces from with
the information in context.

1204
00:54:24,960 --> 00:54:27,260
And so it's basically
it gets a higher number

1205
00:54:27,260 --> 00:54:30,660
of reasoning traces sampled
that could hit on the answer

1206
00:54:30,660 --> 00:54:31,780
to the question.

1207
00:54:31,780 --> 00:54:36,500
And so, for example,
on the syllogism tasks,

1208
00:54:36,500 --> 00:54:39,180
we find that the
models generalize or do

1209
00:54:39,180 --> 00:54:41,940
better on the syllogistic tests
with the augmented fine-tuning

1210
00:54:41,940 --> 00:54:43,690
than they do from the
in-context learning.

1211
00:54:43,690 --> 00:54:45,460
And that's my
explanation for it,

1212
00:54:45,460 --> 00:54:47,740
is that you're
getting the model to

1213
00:54:47,740 --> 00:54:52,340
by generating more reasoning
traces, its more likely to chain

1214
00:54:52,340 --> 00:54:54,900
together the right statements
to make some inference it

1215
00:54:54,900 --> 00:54:56,180
needs to make.

1216
00:54:56,180 --> 00:54:57,587
Professor [INAUDIBLE].

1217
00:54:57,587 --> 00:55:03,220

1218
00:55:03,220 --> 00:55:11,900
Yeah, but, I mean, well,
I guess, in general,

1219
00:55:11,900 --> 00:55:14,780
any question you want to ask
the model that you don't provide

1220
00:55:14,780 --> 00:55:16,760
the information in
context at test time,

1221
00:55:16,760 --> 00:55:19,900
that information has to come
from reasoning about things

1222
00:55:19,900 --> 00:55:21,200
from the training corpus.

1223
00:55:21,200 --> 00:55:24,420
Otherwise, there's no way the
model could figure it out.

1224
00:55:24,420 --> 00:55:26,084
I have a quick question.

1225
00:55:26,084 --> 00:55:26,820
Sure.

1226
00:55:26,820 --> 00:55:27,787
OK.

1227
00:55:27,787 --> 00:55:29,620
The first one is, if I
understood correctly,

1228
00:55:29,620 --> 00:55:31,780
in pre-training, you
have two facts, like fish

1229
00:55:31,780 --> 00:55:33,080
lay eggs and chicken lay eggs.

1230
00:55:33,080 --> 00:55:33,580
Yeah.

1231
00:55:33,580 --> 00:55:35,497
And the inference time
you go fish and chicken

1232
00:55:35,497 --> 00:55:37,540
are like each other
in that point.

1233
00:55:37,540 --> 00:55:40,700
It will never see the eggs.

1234
00:55:40,700 --> 00:55:41,460
I think it--

1235
00:55:41,460 --> 00:55:43,840
That may be a bold statement,
but it doesn't generalize.

1236
00:55:43,840 --> 00:55:47,100
I think it would actually
probably generalize

1237
00:55:47,100 --> 00:55:52,960
that because it's
more of a forward--

1238
00:55:52,960 --> 00:55:54,880
at least if it
saw some examples.

1239
00:55:54,880 --> 00:55:55,540
[INAUDIBLE]

1240
00:55:55,540 --> 00:55:57,820
But let's say you have two
statements like that which

1241
00:55:57,820 --> 00:56:00,820
didn't sound so rhythmic,
like they sound same,

1242
00:56:00,820 --> 00:56:04,500
you use different words
for X, but it would never

1243
00:56:04,500 --> 00:56:08,060
actually fill in that-- it's
unlikely to fill in that gap

1244
00:56:08,060 --> 00:56:08,600
basically.

1245
00:56:08,600 --> 00:56:11,300
Yeah, so I think
it depends a lot

1246
00:56:11,300 --> 00:56:13,052
on the statistics of
the training corpus,

1247
00:56:13,052 --> 00:56:13,760
just to be clear.

1248
00:56:13,760 --> 00:56:16,580
I showed the example of
the word co-occurrences

1249
00:56:16,580 --> 00:56:18,920
can support this in certain
kinds of situations.

1250
00:56:18,920 --> 00:56:23,148
But assuming-- there are at
least some situations in which I

1251
00:56:23,148 --> 00:56:24,440
agree it won't generalize that.

1252
00:56:24,440 --> 00:56:27,420
So my next question,
then, is in [INAUDIBLE]--

1253
00:56:27,420 --> 00:56:29,170
and maybe you thought of this.

1254
00:56:29,170 --> 00:56:29,920
Maybe you haven't.

1255
00:56:29,920 --> 00:56:33,980
You could train the model on
completely abstract sequences,

1256
00:56:33,980 --> 00:56:37,020
and it would get all the
generalization capability

1257
00:56:37,020 --> 00:56:39,380
it could ever get
in the parameters.

1258
00:56:39,380 --> 00:56:39,940
Yeah.

1259
00:56:39,940 --> 00:56:43,420
So you have a copyright
free pre-training corpus

1260
00:56:43,420 --> 00:56:46,460
that's made up of nonsense
words, where maybe

1261
00:56:46,460 --> 00:56:47,760
the tokens will match up then.

1262
00:56:47,760 --> 00:56:49,070
But, yeah.

1263
00:56:49,070 --> 00:56:54,090
Yeah, so what I want
to say about that is

1264
00:56:54,090 --> 00:56:56,223
I think there is some
mileage to be gained

1265
00:56:56,223 --> 00:56:57,390
from doing things like this.

1266
00:56:57,390 --> 00:56:59,810
And there's various papers
that have shown that you can,

1267
00:56:59,810 --> 00:57:03,470
for example, pre-trained
models on formal languages

1268
00:57:03,470 --> 00:57:06,650
and you can get some
improvements in how

1269
00:57:06,650 --> 00:57:09,490
they learn on real language.

1270
00:57:09,490 --> 00:57:13,055
The problem is that,
as I said in the answer

1271
00:57:13,055 --> 00:57:14,430
to some of the
earlier questions,

1272
00:57:14,430 --> 00:57:17,450
I actually think the statistical
structure is a very good thing

1273
00:57:17,450 --> 00:57:18,550
to be learning.

1274
00:57:18,550 --> 00:57:21,870
And so, for example, in some
of the earlier work we've done,

1275
00:57:21,870 --> 00:57:24,350
we've looked at content
effects on reasoning.

1276
00:57:24,350 --> 00:57:27,878
So how language models reason
about logical structures

1277
00:57:27,878 --> 00:57:30,170
is very dependent on the
information and the structures

1278
00:57:30,170 --> 00:57:31,390
they're reasoning about.

1279
00:57:31,390 --> 00:57:33,138
Turns out that that's
true of humans too

1280
00:57:33,138 --> 00:57:34,930
and it's true of humans
and language models

1281
00:57:34,930 --> 00:57:38,090
in the same ways across
some classic problems that

1282
00:57:38,090 --> 00:57:40,690
have been studied in humans.

1283
00:57:40,690 --> 00:57:43,850
And the way I think
about it and the way

1284
00:57:43,850 --> 00:57:46,610
we wrote about it in that paper
is that the reason for this

1285
00:57:46,610 --> 00:57:50,010
is actually that it's like
normatively a good thing, if you

1286
00:57:50,010 --> 00:57:52,070
want to make accurate
inferences in the world,

1287
00:57:52,070 --> 00:57:54,130
to use all the information
that you can in

1288
00:57:54,130 --> 00:57:55,870
order to make those inferences.

1289
00:57:55,870 --> 00:57:58,730
And so logical
reasoning is hard to do

1290
00:57:58,730 --> 00:58:00,610
and integrating a
bunch of statistics

1291
00:58:00,610 --> 00:58:04,270
is relatively easy and natural
for a parametric learner to do,

1292
00:58:04,270 --> 00:58:08,710
it makes sense for the system to
not always rely purely on logic,

1293
00:58:08,710 --> 00:58:10,710
but to actually use that
statistical structure.

1294
00:58:10,710 --> 00:58:14,210
And that will actually help it
to generalize in expectation.

1295
00:58:14,210 --> 00:58:20,970
And so I think that if you try
to make a system that's just not

1296
00:58:20,970 --> 00:58:24,490
using the statistics
but just generalizing

1297
00:58:24,490 --> 00:58:27,650
on the basis of structure
alone, for the same reason

1298
00:58:27,650 --> 00:58:32,170
that [? guffi ?] just
wasn't a tractable approach

1299
00:58:32,170 --> 00:58:34,650
to AI in real-world
problems where you just have

1300
00:58:34,650 --> 00:58:37,410
too many things you could
possibly make inferences about,

1301
00:58:37,410 --> 00:58:39,050
you need that
statistical structure

1302
00:58:39,050 --> 00:58:40,770
to constrain the
kinds of inferences

1303
00:58:40,770 --> 00:58:42,590
that you want the
system to make.

1304
00:58:42,590 --> 00:58:44,730
That's what allows the
system to efficiently get

1305
00:58:44,730 --> 00:58:46,660
to a feasible answer.

1306
00:58:46,660 --> 00:58:49,440
And I think that's important for
both understanding why humans

1307
00:58:49,440 --> 00:58:53,020
reason in the quirky and weird
ways that we do sometimes

1308
00:58:53,020 --> 00:58:56,220
and for understanding
what language models need.

1309
00:58:56,220 --> 00:58:58,840
So I think that you would
need the models to pick up

1310
00:58:58,840 --> 00:59:01,400
that statistical
structure at some point.

1311
00:59:01,400 --> 00:59:02,480
Yes.

1312
00:59:02,480 --> 00:59:04,860
My question is related to that.

1313
00:59:04,860 --> 00:59:06,880
But firstly, thank
you for your talk.

1314
00:59:06,880 --> 00:59:11,160
Secondly, at the end,
you made a comparison

1315
00:59:11,160 --> 00:59:14,640
between the hippocampus and
neocortex and then in-context

1316
00:59:14,640 --> 00:59:17,240
learning and
parametric learning.

1317
00:59:17,240 --> 00:59:20,600
My first question then
is what do you think is,

1318
00:59:20,600 --> 00:59:24,760
from what you've seen,
the coolest similarity

1319
00:59:24,760 --> 00:59:29,120
between human brains and natural
learning and machine learning?

1320
00:59:29,120 --> 00:59:33,200
And then secondly, where exactly
do you think this metaphor

1321
00:59:33,200 --> 00:59:34,680
breaks down?

1322
00:59:34,680 --> 00:59:35,880
Yeah, OK.

1323
00:59:35,880 --> 00:59:36,900
Great question.

1324
00:59:36,900 --> 00:59:50,160
So I think I'm going
to say that there's

1325
00:59:50,160 --> 00:59:57,640
been a lot of interesting
work comparing language model

1326
00:59:57,640 --> 01:00:02,760
transformer-style attention to
hippocampal memory, not just

1327
01:00:02,760 --> 01:00:03,260
our work.

1328
01:00:03,260 --> 01:00:06,560
Although, this takes a
different perspective on it.

1329
01:00:06,560 --> 01:00:08,700
And James Whittington,
for example,

1330
01:00:08,700 --> 01:00:11,040
has done a bunch of
work in this space.

1331
01:00:11,040 --> 01:00:16,440
And I think that analogy
is very interesting

1332
01:00:16,440 --> 01:00:18,240
because at some
level, you wouldn't

1333
01:00:18,240 --> 01:00:21,200
think at all that
transformer-style attention is

1334
01:00:21,200 --> 01:00:23,960
doing anything
like what we think

1335
01:00:23,960 --> 01:00:25,300
of the hippocampus is doing.

1336
01:00:25,300 --> 01:00:28,120
But it is like
pulling information

1337
01:00:28,120 --> 01:00:30,800
out from a long
sequence of past states

1338
01:00:30,800 --> 01:00:34,100
according to some of weighted
similarity-based lookup.

1339
01:00:34,100 --> 01:00:36,480
And when you phrase it in that
way, it's like some things

1340
01:00:36,480 --> 01:00:38,420
that people have thought about
the hippocampus as doing.

1341
01:00:38,420 --> 01:00:40,045
And James Whittington,
who I mentioned,

1342
01:00:40,045 --> 01:00:42,600
has worked on this
analogy quite a bit.

1343
01:00:42,600 --> 01:00:48,080
So I think that is an
interesting example of how

1344
01:00:48,080 --> 01:00:51,520
you can have systems that are
clearly at the implementation

1345
01:00:51,520 --> 01:00:52,820
level completely different.

1346
01:00:52,820 --> 01:00:54,300
Whatever the
hippocampus is doing,

1347
01:00:54,300 --> 01:00:59,820
it's definitely not doing
transformer-style query value

1348
01:00:59,820 --> 01:01:00,900
attention.

1349
01:01:00,900 --> 01:01:04,720
But that turned out to have some
similar computational properties

1350
01:01:04,720 --> 01:01:07,540
in some ways that can make one
system a good model for another.

1351
01:01:07,540 --> 01:01:11,040
So I think that's just
cool as a, I don't know,

1352
01:01:11,040 --> 01:01:15,400
example of the separation
of computational and

1353
01:01:15,400 --> 01:01:21,720
implementational levels in doing
cognitive science, for example.

1354
01:01:21,720 --> 01:01:24,200
Yeah, maybe I'll go
with that as the answer

1355
01:01:24,200 --> 01:01:25,260
to your first question.

1356
01:01:25,260 --> 01:01:26,510
Where the analogy breaks down.

1357
01:01:26,510 --> 01:01:29,440
Well, I already said the
implementation is obviously

1358
01:01:29,440 --> 01:01:30,940
the place that breaks down.

1359
01:01:30,940 --> 01:01:37,790
But the other place that I think
it breaks down more is that--

1360
01:01:37,790 --> 01:01:41,760
let's imagine that you had a--

1361
01:01:41,760 --> 01:01:43,930
well, yeah, it's complicated.

1362
01:01:43,930 --> 01:01:45,390
But, I mean,
basically, you can't

1363
01:01:45,390 --> 01:01:47,230
fit a whole lifetime
of experience

1364
01:01:47,230 --> 01:01:50,310
into a language model's context.

1365
01:01:50,310 --> 01:01:51,790
That's not tractable.

1366
01:01:51,790 --> 01:01:55,248
And yet, the hippocampus
in combination

1367
01:01:55,248 --> 01:01:56,790
with the cortex,
because it turns out

1368
01:01:56,790 --> 01:01:59,093
there's actually quite
complicated interactions,

1369
01:01:59,093 --> 01:02:01,510
and the hippocampus isn't
really storing episodic memories

1370
01:02:01,510 --> 01:02:04,210
for your whole life, certainly
not independently anyway,

1371
01:02:04,210 --> 01:02:09,830
but the hippocampus is able to
preserve a much larger array

1372
01:02:09,830 --> 01:02:11,150
of information.

1373
01:02:11,150 --> 01:02:13,610
One of probably consequences
of that, I think,

1374
01:02:13,610 --> 01:02:18,190
is that the hippocampus is much
more likely-- at least compared

1375
01:02:18,190 --> 01:02:19,630
to the scale of
information we're

1376
01:02:19,630 --> 01:02:22,310
putting in the transformers
now, the hippocampus

1377
01:02:22,310 --> 01:02:26,370
does much more
generative retrieval.

1378
01:02:26,370 --> 01:02:29,630
Most of the-- you
may have seen some

1379
01:02:29,630 --> 01:02:32,670
of these stats that
many things you remember

1380
01:02:32,670 --> 01:02:35,990
as episodic memories
didn't actually happen,

1381
01:02:35,990 --> 01:02:39,430
a non-trivial percentage of
them didn't actually happen.

1382
01:02:39,430 --> 01:02:41,970
And you've just
constructed that memory.

1383
01:02:41,970 --> 01:02:44,130
And then each time
you think about it,

1384
01:02:44,130 --> 01:02:48,050
it becomes a stronger
encoding of that memory.

1385
01:02:48,050 --> 01:02:49,510
So there's a lot
of confabulation

1386
01:02:49,510 --> 01:02:50,630
of the hippocampus.

1387
01:02:50,630 --> 01:02:53,490
That does happen with
in-context learning too.

1388
01:02:53,490 --> 01:02:55,850
But I think on the
scales that we have,

1389
01:02:55,850 --> 01:03:01,790
it's maybe a lower rate actually
than it is for the hippocampus.

1390
01:03:01,790 --> 01:03:04,190
But the flip side
of that is that I

1391
01:03:04,190 --> 01:03:07,110
think that perhaps
the generative nature

1392
01:03:07,110 --> 01:03:10,390
of the hippocampus is
more flexible in some ways

1393
01:03:10,390 --> 01:03:13,270
than transformer
attention is or is

1394
01:03:13,270 --> 01:03:16,830
able to do some more
sophisticated kinds

1395
01:03:16,830 --> 01:03:18,010
of generalization.

1396
01:03:18,010 --> 01:03:21,110
And so those are a few different
ways that the analogy breaks

1397
01:03:21,110 --> 01:03:23,990
down, I think.

1398
01:03:23,990 --> 01:03:25,250
Maybe I'll go to you since--

1399
01:03:25,250 --> 01:03:25,750
Hi.

1400
01:03:25,750 --> 01:03:29,410
I'm building a behavioral
constitution for AI,

1401
01:03:29,410 --> 01:03:31,910
which [INAUDIBLE]
system prompts.

1402
01:03:31,910 --> 01:03:35,450
I was wondering, for
behavioral reliability,

1403
01:03:35,450 --> 01:03:39,542
do you think context will
ever match the [INAUDIBLE]?

1404
01:03:39,542 --> 01:03:42,670

1405
01:03:42,670 --> 01:03:43,870
Yeah, it's a good question.

1406
01:03:43,870 --> 01:03:52,830
I think it depends
on what you mean.

1407
01:03:52,830 --> 01:03:55,710
Because I think
usually when people

1408
01:03:55,710 --> 01:04:00,370
think about a
constitution, for example,

1409
01:04:00,370 --> 01:04:02,790
shaping a model's
behavior, it's like you

1410
01:04:02,790 --> 01:04:06,590
want this model when it's
placed in any new situation

1411
01:04:06,590 --> 01:04:08,730
to behave in accordance
with the constitution.

1412
01:04:08,730 --> 01:04:11,670
And the kinds of
experiments I showed

1413
01:04:11,670 --> 01:04:13,550
you are things
where the model gets

1414
01:04:13,550 --> 01:04:16,210
a bunch of consistent
information in context

1415
01:04:16,210 --> 01:04:18,992
and then it's able to
use that effectively.

1416
01:04:18,992 --> 01:04:20,950
I think the models have
a much harder time when

1417
01:04:20,950 --> 01:04:24,190
they have inconsistent
information in context.

1418
01:04:24,190 --> 01:04:26,590
For example, if you
have a constitution that

1419
01:04:26,590 --> 01:04:29,590
says one thing and then you
have a user that's demanding you

1420
01:04:29,590 --> 01:04:32,070
to do another thing,
no, my grandma is dying

1421
01:04:32,070 --> 01:04:34,070
and I really need you to
hack into this computer

1422
01:04:34,070 --> 01:04:40,420
so you can help me to help
her, it's harder for the models

1423
01:04:40,420 --> 01:04:43,700
to reconcile those different
sources of information.

1424
01:04:43,700 --> 01:04:48,680
And I think because of
that, I think this is--

1425
01:04:48,680 --> 01:04:51,300
I'm not necessarily
saying that in-context

1426
01:04:51,300 --> 01:04:54,560
will be better or worse than
parametric learning there.

1427
01:04:54,560 --> 01:04:58,100
I think that in either case,
it can be hard for the model

1428
01:04:58,100 --> 01:05:00,480
to reconcile those different
sorts of information,

1429
01:05:00,480 --> 01:05:03,100
like the things that it learned
as parameters, the things that

1430
01:05:03,100 --> 01:05:05,517
are in context, and the things
that the user is telling it

1431
01:05:05,517 --> 01:05:06,700
right now.

1432
01:05:06,700 --> 01:05:10,420
And because of that, it's
hard to guarantee reliability

1433
01:05:10,420 --> 01:05:13,500
of either of these
methods, I think.

1434
01:05:13,500 --> 01:05:15,860
In some sense,
because the models

1435
01:05:15,860 --> 01:05:18,500
are such good
in-context learners,

1436
01:05:18,500 --> 01:05:22,360
it's very hard to stop them from
adapting to some weird context

1437
01:05:22,360 --> 01:05:24,780
the user gets them.

1438
01:05:24,780 --> 01:05:26,100
Yes.

1439
01:05:26,100 --> 01:05:30,140
So I think this stuff
is really interesting.

1440
01:05:30,140 --> 01:05:39,540
[INAUDIBLE] how much you should
invest in training [INAUDIBLE]

1441
01:05:39,540 --> 01:05:40,780
parameter?

1442
01:05:40,780 --> 01:05:43,500
And how much of it can
be offset or assisted

1443
01:05:43,500 --> 01:05:45,460
by actually
in-contrast learning?

1444
01:05:45,460 --> 01:05:47,400
Do you know [INAUDIBLE]?

1445
01:05:47,400 --> 01:05:53,100

1446
01:05:53,100 --> 01:05:54,640
Yeah, that's a good question.

1447
01:05:54,640 --> 01:06:01,560
I haven't published
anything on those topics.

1448
01:06:01,560 --> 01:06:07,940
But there's been some
work, for example--

1449
01:06:07,940 --> 01:06:13,195
this is a slightly,
OK, this is slightly

1450
01:06:13,195 --> 01:06:14,320
a tangent to your question.

1451
01:06:14,320 --> 01:06:17,940
But I'll just read it as an
example of the kinds of things

1452
01:06:17,940 --> 01:06:18,680
in this space.

1453
01:06:18,680 --> 01:06:21,620
There is for a while
this notion of chinchilla

1454
01:06:21,620 --> 01:06:24,740
optimal language
model scaling, which

1455
01:06:24,740 --> 01:06:29,140
was about for a given fixed
training budget, how much

1456
01:06:29,140 --> 01:06:31,980
should you train your model to
get an optimally good model out

1457
01:06:31,980 --> 01:06:33,020
at the end?

1458
01:06:33,020 --> 01:06:36,810
And then people realized
later that if you're

1459
01:06:36,810 --> 01:06:40,362
going to do a bunch of inference
on your model at test time,

1460
01:06:40,362 --> 01:06:42,570
well, you don't just care
about the training compute.

1461
01:06:42,570 --> 01:06:45,490
You also care about how good
the model is at test time

1462
01:06:45,490 --> 01:06:46,830
relative to its compute.

1463
01:06:46,830 --> 01:06:50,570
And that changes the
scaling laws you should fit

1464
01:06:50,570 --> 01:06:53,450
for how big of a model you
should train for a given

1465
01:06:53,450 --> 01:06:54,850
amount of training compute.

1466
01:06:54,850 --> 01:06:58,730
And so similarly,
I think there are

1467
01:06:58,730 --> 01:07:00,490
scaling laws you
can fit that trade

1468
01:07:00,490 --> 01:07:02,570
off these various
parameters, like how much do

1469
01:07:02,570 --> 01:07:03,630
you want to augment your data?

1470
01:07:03,630 --> 01:07:05,172
How much do you want
to get new data?

1471
01:07:05,172 --> 01:07:07,330
How much do you want to do
compute at training time

1472
01:07:07,330 --> 01:07:08,770
versus at test time?

1473
01:07:08,770 --> 01:07:12,610
And that will depend
a lot on what exactly

1474
01:07:12,610 --> 01:07:14,230
you're trying to get
the system to do.

1475
01:07:14,230 --> 01:07:15,070
At what cost?

1476
01:07:15,070 --> 01:07:17,130
At what time?

1477
01:07:17,130 --> 01:07:22,970
So I'm not sure if I've seen
a paper that explicitly tries

1478
01:07:22,970 --> 01:07:24,430
to trade those things off.

1479
01:07:24,430 --> 01:07:28,130
But I'm quite confident
that some people out there

1480
01:07:28,130 --> 01:07:32,570
are thinking about those issues,
at least at one of the labs,

1481
01:07:32,570 --> 01:07:33,630
if not all of them.

1482
01:07:33,630 --> 01:07:36,170

1483
01:07:36,170 --> 01:07:37,330
Yes.

1484
01:07:37,330 --> 01:07:40,550
So you talked about offline
augmenting the training data.

1485
01:07:40,550 --> 01:07:42,720
Did you look into
lifting yourself

1486
01:07:42,720 --> 01:07:44,970
up from your own bootstraps
or making the model better

1487
01:07:44,970 --> 01:07:47,270
without much information?

1488
01:07:47,270 --> 01:07:50,390
Yeah, you probe what the
trade offs are of doing that.

1489
01:07:50,390 --> 01:07:52,730
What does it get
worse in, if anything.

1490
01:07:52,730 --> 01:07:54,670
Yeah, so I think--

1491
01:07:54,670 --> 01:07:58,330

1492
01:07:58,330 --> 01:08:01,990
OK, I'll say a few
things about this.

1493
01:08:01,990 --> 01:08:05,190
So one trade off is
just the inference cost.

1494
01:08:05,190 --> 01:08:09,050
And you should actually think
that that's non-trivial.

1495
01:08:09,050 --> 01:08:13,210
It's a lot more expensive to
do this offline augmentation

1496
01:08:13,210 --> 01:08:16,609
than just to do a fine-tuning
pass over a small data set.

1497
01:08:16,609 --> 01:08:19,229
I don't know exactly
the trade off,

1498
01:08:19,229 --> 01:08:21,770
but I would guess at least
10 times as expensive

1499
01:08:21,770 --> 01:08:25,170
because you have to do a bunch
of inference/generation passes,

1500
01:08:25,170 --> 01:08:27,870
then you have to do a training
pass over a larger data set.

1501
01:08:27,870 --> 01:08:29,910
And so it's a lot
more expensive.

1502
01:08:29,910 --> 01:08:31,970
And that's one of
the major trade offs.

1503
01:08:31,970 --> 01:08:41,609
I think that you will
get some hallucination

1504
01:08:41,609 --> 01:08:43,410
in the augmented data.

1505
01:08:43,410 --> 01:08:47,810
And in our examples, this
was at a low enough rate

1506
01:08:47,810 --> 01:08:50,569
that it still worth it to
keep augmenting the data

1507
01:08:50,569 --> 01:08:53,090
because, well, I mean,
one way of looking at it

1508
01:08:53,090 --> 01:08:55,359
is that if you
get hallucinations

1509
01:08:55,359 --> 01:08:57,609
some percent of the time and
you get the right answers

1510
01:08:57,609 --> 01:09:00,151
more percent of the time, well,
as long as the hallucinations

1511
01:09:00,151 --> 01:09:03,729
are independent from
each other, then

1512
01:09:03,729 --> 01:09:06,830
the model will on average
learn the right answer.

1513
01:09:06,830 --> 01:09:09,810
I think that's one of the
factors that goes into it.

1514
01:09:09,810 --> 01:09:14,410
I think when you do fine-tuning
on a larger data set,

1515
01:09:14,410 --> 01:09:17,029
if you don't change
anything else,

1516
01:09:17,029 --> 01:09:19,930
you will probably distort
the model's knowledge

1517
01:09:19,930 --> 01:09:22,330
of other things more.

1518
01:09:22,330 --> 01:09:25,410
And so that's a factor that you
definitely have to consider.

1519
01:09:25,410 --> 01:09:28,630
Most fine-tuning methods,
including the ones we used here,

1520
01:09:28,630 --> 01:09:31,569
have some kind of
regularization to try

1521
01:09:31,569 --> 01:09:33,560
to prevent the model
from drifting too

1522
01:09:33,560 --> 01:09:36,240
far from the original model.

1523
01:09:36,240 --> 01:09:38,580
And that will tend
to counteract that.

1524
01:09:38,580 --> 01:09:41,000
But how exactly those trade
offs depend on how exactly

1525
01:09:41,000 --> 01:09:43,340
you're doing the regularization
and stuff like that.

1526
01:09:43,340 --> 01:09:43,840
A follow-up.

1527
01:09:43,840 --> 01:09:46,160
Did you feel very
satisfied with your choice

1528
01:09:46,160 --> 01:09:49,640
of prompt for each generation
of the augmented training data?

1529
01:09:49,640 --> 01:09:52,399
Is you want to maybe have
some foreknowledge of the task

1530
01:09:52,399 --> 01:09:54,260
you're trying to improve
its performance on.

1531
01:09:54,260 --> 01:09:54,760
Yeah.

1532
01:09:54,760 --> 01:09:56,320
So you're cheating a little bit.

1533
01:09:56,320 --> 01:09:56,520
Yeah.

1534
01:09:56,520 --> 01:09:58,520
You're cheating to the
point where you literally

1535
01:09:58,520 --> 01:09:59,440
told it the answers.

1536
01:09:59,440 --> 01:10:01,648
You have to choose something
in between those points.

1537
01:10:01,648 --> 01:10:03,960
Is it satisfying to understand?

1538
01:10:03,960 --> 01:10:07,480
Yeah, so we did use
the same prompts

1539
01:10:07,480 --> 01:10:09,300
across the different
experiments.

1540
01:10:09,300 --> 01:10:14,200
We did ablation on the prompts,
and it actually mattered less

1541
01:10:14,200 --> 01:10:16,060
than we thought it would.

1542
01:10:16,060 --> 01:10:18,320
But I think if you were--

1543
01:10:18,320 --> 01:10:21,760
the experiments in that paper
were all about factual knowledge

1544
01:10:21,760 --> 01:10:24,280
of some kind, whether it's
reversals, or syllogisms,

1545
01:10:24,280 --> 01:10:25,420
or things like that.

1546
01:10:25,420 --> 01:10:27,720
I think if you were like,
now I want to test this out

1547
01:10:27,720 --> 01:10:30,160
on a math reasoning
data set, you

1548
01:10:30,160 --> 01:10:33,080
might find that those
prompts don't actually

1549
01:10:33,080 --> 01:10:36,200
work that well there.

1550
01:10:36,200 --> 01:10:44,720
And so I think it's an
important general question how

1551
01:10:44,720 --> 01:10:47,820
much the prompts--

1552
01:10:47,820 --> 01:10:51,000
how to write prompts that are
sufficiently general that they

1553
01:10:51,000 --> 01:10:56,040
could cover anything or how
to adapt prompts to the data

1554
01:10:56,040 --> 01:10:57,300
that you're augmenting.

1555
01:10:57,300 --> 01:10:58,758
Is there a prompt
you could tell us

1556
01:10:58,758 --> 01:11:01,260
that you used for this
reversal reasoning syllogism.

1557
01:11:01,260 --> 01:11:04,560
Yeah, so the prompts
that-- you can see them

1558
01:11:04,560 --> 01:11:06,262
in the appendix of the paper.

1559
01:11:06,262 --> 01:11:07,220
Been a couple of years.

1560
01:11:07,220 --> 01:11:08,970
So I don't remember
exactly what they are.

1561
01:11:08,970 --> 01:11:12,760
But they are of the character
here's a bunch of documents.

1562
01:11:12,760 --> 01:11:15,480
Now here's a
particular document.

1563
01:11:15,480 --> 01:11:20,720
Can you rewrite this document
and write any connections

1564
01:11:20,720 --> 01:11:23,080
that you can make between
this document and other ones

1565
01:11:23,080 --> 01:11:25,480
in the corpus, including
rephrasing things and thinking

1566
01:11:25,480 --> 01:11:27,780
about relations between
different entities and so on?

1567
01:11:27,780 --> 01:11:29,560
Something like that.

1568
01:11:29,560 --> 01:11:35,990
And there's been other methods
like Tatsu's group here

1569
01:11:35,990 --> 01:11:37,870
has done some things
along these lines

1570
01:11:37,870 --> 01:11:39,250
and various other groups have.

1571
01:11:39,250 --> 01:11:42,630
So you can see an
array of prompts

1572
01:11:42,630 --> 01:11:44,530
across the literature
on how to do this.

1573
01:11:44,530 --> 01:11:49,470
But I think they're all--
our prompts are quite

1574
01:11:49,470 --> 01:11:52,150
tied to the factual
and entities setting.

1575
01:11:52,150 --> 01:11:57,010
And doing this in a fully
general way is a hard problem.

1576
01:11:57,010 --> 01:11:58,070
You were very indirect.

1577
01:11:58,070 --> 01:12:00,130
Yeah, I mean, we tried not to--

1578
01:12:00,130 --> 01:12:04,070
[INAUDIBLE] much on
logical reasoning.

1579
01:12:04,070 --> 01:12:06,270
Yeah, I mean, we
wanted the reviewers

1580
01:12:06,270 --> 01:12:08,970
to not feel like we were
totally cheating on that.

1581
01:12:08,970 --> 01:12:14,790
But it's hard to
do it perfectly.

1582
01:12:14,790 --> 01:12:17,523
Unfortunately, that's all
the time we have for today.

1583
01:12:17,523 --> 01:12:19,690
But thanks, everyone, for
your insightful questions.

1584
01:12:19,690 --> 01:12:21,357
And let's give another
round of applause

1585
01:12:21,357 --> 01:12:23,128
to our speaker for today.

1586
01:12:23,128 --> 01:12:24,170
Thank you all for coming.

1587
01:12:24,170 --> 01:12:27,520
[APPLAUSE]

1588
01:12:27,520 --> 01:12:30,000
